;=======================================================================
; For God so loved the world, that he gave his only begotten Son, that
; whosoever believeth in him should not perish, but have everlasting
; life.
;                                              - John 3:16, KJV
;=======================================================================
; EGT80 - "Ethernet Gateway Terminal"
;
; A CP/M terminal program for the Ethernet Gateway, written for real Z80
; hardware as much as the gateway's own CP/M emulator.  Unlike the period
; terminals it replaces, the serial port is chosen at RUN TIME from a menu
; instead of being fixed when the program is assembled - one EGT80.COM
; serves an RC2014, an Altair, an SC126, or the emulator.
;
; Ricky Bryce.  Part of the Ethernet Gateway project.
;
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; ASSEMBLER COMPATIBILITY
;
; This source is written to assemble with PERIOD tooling - SLR Z80ASM
; (Z80ASM EGT80/A), Microsoft M80, and ZMAC - so it can be rebuilt on a
; real CP/M machine, not just cross-assembled.  That imposes rules;
; please keep to them when editing:
;
;   - No macros, no conditional assembly, no local/temporary labels.
;   - Upper-case Zilog mnemonics, one statement per line.
;   - Labels unique within their first SIX characters.
;   - Directives limited to EQU, ORG, DB, DW, DS, END.
;   - Expressions limited to + - * / AND OR SHL SHR and '$'.
;   - Plain 7-bit ASCII, no tabs inside quoted strings, <= 80 columns.
;   - CRLF line endings (the Makefile stages them; an LF-only file makes
;     M80 and ZMAC assemble almost nothing while reporting success).
;
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; COMPLETE: all five port families (Z80 SIO/2, 6850 ACIA, RomWBW HBIOS,
; Z180 ASCI, CP/M AUX:), the two-step port menu, settings saved into the
; program's own file, and XMODEM file transfer in both directions.
;
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; HOW THE HOT PATH IS KEPT CHEAP
;
; Two indirections, both resolved before the loop runs:
;
;   Port    - four RAM vectors (VPST/VPIN/VPOST/VPOUT) are rewritten when
;             the user picks a port, so selecting a port costs nothing per
;             byte.  This is the shape QTERM's overlays use, except ours
;             is chosen at run time rather than at assembly time.
;   Console - three RAM vectors (VCST/VCIN/VCOUT) are pointed either at
;             the BIOS console entries (CP/M 2.2, far cheaper per byte
;             than a BDOS call) or at BDOS-6 wrappers (CP/M 3), decided
;             once at startup.  The loop never tests the version.
;
; The terminal loop deliberately keeps NO state in registers across those
; calls: everything it needs is in A or in memory.  That removes any need
; for the vectors to preserve registers - which matters because on CP/M 3
; they land in the BDOS, which preserves nothing.
;
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; REGISTER CONVENTION - read this before adding a counted loop
;
; The XMODEM code counts 128 bytes in B while calling routines that need
; counters of their own, and three of them (PSEND, XGETB, XACCUM) used BC
; for that.  Each one silently destroyed the caller's count, and the
; symptom was not subtle: the send loop walked off the end of the buffer
; and shovelled a megabyte of RAM at the wire.
;
; So: PSEND and XGETB preserve BC; XACCUM preserves BC and HL (the block
; loops walk the buffer with HL while it needs HL for the accumulators);
; COUT/CST/CIN preserve BC, DE and HL.  The port and console vectors
; preserve NOTHING - call them only with everything you care about either
; in A or in memory.
;
; And the order matters as much as the pushes: a routine that restores a
; register BEFORE its last call to a port vector has not restored it at
; all, because the driver sets B to address the port.  PSEND had exactly
; that bug - it popped BC and then jumped to the output vector - and the
; symptom was the same runaway buffer walk as forgetting the push.
;=======================================================================

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; CP/M system equates
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
BDOS    EQU     0005H           ; BDOS entry point
WBOOT   EQU     0000H           ; warm boot (exit to CCP)
BIOSPT  EQU     0001H           ; word: address of the BIOS WBOOT entry
BCONOU  EQU     2               ; console output (no reserved values)
BCONIO  EQU     6               ; direct console I/O (CP/M 2.2 and 3)
BVERS   EQU     12              ; return CP/M version
BOPEN   EQU     15              ; open file
BCLOSE  EQU     16              ; close file
BSETDMA EQU     26              ; set the DMA (record buffer) address
BWRRAN  EQU     34              ; write random record
BRDBUF  EQU     10              ; read console buffer (a line)
BDELET  EQU     19              ; delete file
BREADS  EQU     20              ; read sequential record
BWRITS  EQU     21              ; write sequential record
BMAKE   EQU     22              ; create file
BSEARF  EQU     17              ; search for first match

; Direct-console-I/O sub-codes used on CP/M 3
DCIRD   EQU     0FFH            ; read a character
DCIST   EQU     0FEH            ; console status

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; Character equates
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
BEL     EQU     07H
BS      EQU     08H
TAB     EQU     09H
LF      EQU     0AH
CR      EQU     0DH
ESC     EQU     1BH
DEL     EQU     7FH

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; XMODEM protocol bytes and bounds
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
SOH     EQU     01H             ; start of a 128-byte block
EOT     EQU     04H             ; end of transmission
ACK     EQU     06H             ; block accepted
NAK     EQU     15H             ; block rejected / send in checksum mode
CAN     EQU     18H             ; cancel (two in a row)
CRCC    EQU     'C'             ; receiver asking for CRC mode
XRETRY  EQU     10              ; retries per block, and 'C' attempts
XNOISE  EQU     255             ; junk bytes tolerated before a transfer
XSHORT  EQU     1               ; inter-byte wait, in XGETB passes
XLONG   EQU     6               ; wait for a block / handshake

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; Port kinds.  Only SIO is implemented in phase 1; the rest are listed
; here because the settings block records the kind numerically and the
; numbering must not shift when they arrive.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
PKNONE  EQU     0               ; nothing selected
PKSIO   EQU     1               ; Z80 SIO/2
PKACIA  EQU     2               ; 6850 ACIA        (phase 2)
PKHBIO  EQU     3               ; RomWBW HBIOS     (phase 2)
PKASCI  EQU     4               ; Z180 ASCI        (phase 2)
PKAUX   EQU     5               ; CP/M BDOS AUX    (phase 2)

; Build identity lives in MVER, in the strings, and nowhere else - a
; second copy in an equate only invites the two to drift apart.

        ORG     0100H

;=======================================================================
; ENTRY
;
; The first instruction jumps over the settings block, which sits at a
; FIXED, 128-byte-aligned address so that saving it rewrites one record of
; the file containing settings and nothing else - no risk of writing a
; half-open FCB or a live variable back into the image.  Period software
; put its patch area in exactly this place for exactly this reason.
;=======================================================================
START:  JP      BEGIN

;=======================================================================
; SETTINGS  (record 1 of the file: address 0180H, offset 80H)
;
; Defaults are the values that suit the most machines: an RC2014/RomWBW
; second SIO/2 channel, and ANSI pass-through, which is what the gateway
; offers a terminal that asks for colour.  CFGCHK validates all of this at
; startup and falls back to these defaults if a saved block is damaged.
;=======================================================================
        ORG     0180H
CFGSIG: DB      'EGT80CFG'      ; must match CFGREF or the block is ignored
PKIND:  DB      PKSIO           ; selected port kind
PBASE:  DB      82H             ; SIO/2 ch B, ACIA base, or HBIOS unit
PCHAN:  DB      0               ; Z180 ASCI channel (0 or 1); unused elsewhere
PABASE: DB      0               ; Z180 internal I/O base for the ASCI family.
                                ; Its own byte, NOT shared with PBASE: PBASE
                                ; holds a port address for SIO/ACIA and a unit
                                ; number for HBIOS, and selecting ASCI does not
                                ; otherwise set it - so sharing meant ASCI
                                ; inherited whatever the last family used and
                                ; addressed registers that do not exist.
FLTMOD: DB      0               ; 0 = ANSI pass, 1 = ASCII strip
ECHOFL: DB      0               ; local echo off (the remote echoes)
CRLFFL: DB      0               ; do not add LF to a typed CR
ESCCH:  DB      19H             ; ^Y, as the QTERM builds here use
LBAUD:  DB      5               ; index into RATETB; 5 = 9600
LBITS:  DB      8               ; 7 or 8 data bits
LPAR:   DB      0               ; parity: 0 none, 1 even, 2 odd
LSTOP:  DB      1               ; 1 or 2 stop bits
LADIV:  DB      1               ; 6850 clock divider: 0 = /1, 1 = /16, 2 = /64
LSAPP:  DB      0               ; 0 = never program the port - the default, and
                                ; the state to come back to: the port keeps
                                ; whatever the ROM, the firmware or the OS set
                                ; up, which is the arrangement that already
                                ; works on the machine.  1 = apply the line
                                ; settings above whenever the port is selected.
CLSMOD: DB      0               ; how to clear the screen: 0 = ADM-3A ^Z,
                                ; 1 = ANSI ESC [ 2 J, 2 = don't.  ^Z is the
                                ; default because it is what the gateway's
                                ; own terminal translation understands (it
                                ; renders the clear for whatever the far
                                ; client is, PETSCII included) and what most
                                ; CP/M-era terminals use.  A VT100 wants the
                                ; ANSI form.

CFGADR  EQU     0180H           ; where the block lives
CFGREC  EQU     1               ; ...which is file record 1

        ORG     0200H

BEGIN:  LD      SP,STKTOP       ; our stack, not the CCP's
        CALL    CONSET          ; choose the console I/O path
        CALL    CFGCHK          ; trust the saved settings only if sane
        CALL    PORTST          ; point the port vectors at the default
        JP      MAINLP          ; main menu - which draws the title itself

;=======================================================================
; CONSET - decide how console I/O is done, once.
;
; CP/M 2.2: take the BIOS console entries from page zero.  The word at
; 0001H points at the BIOS WBOOT entry, which is the SECOND slot of the
; jump table, so the console entries sit at fixed offsets from it:
; CONST = +3, CONIN = +6, CONOUT = +9.
;
; CP/M 3: the BIOS is banked and must not be called directly, so use
; BDOS 6 (direct console I/O), which exists on both systems.
;=======================================================================
CONSET: LD      C,BVERS
        CALL    BDOS
        LD      (CPMVER),A
        CP      30H             ; CP/M 3.0 or later?
        JR      NC,CON3
        LD      HL,(BIOSPT)     ; BIOS WBOOT entry
        INC     HL
        INC     HL
        INC     HL              ; CONST
        LD      (VCST+1),HL
        INC     HL
        INC     HL
        INC     HL              ; CONIN
        LD      (VCIN+1),HL
        INC     HL
        INC     HL
        INC     HL              ; CONOUT
        LD      (VCOUT+1),HL
        RET
CON3:   LD      HL,B6ST
        LD      (VCST+1),HL
        LD      HL,B6IN
        LD      (VCIN+1),HL
        LD      HL,B6OUT
        LD      (VCOUT+1),HL
        RET

;=======================================================================
; BDOS-6 console wrappers (the CP/M 3 path).
;
; They present the BIOS calling convention the rest of the program uses:
; status returns A=0 for "no key", input returns the byte in A, output
; takes the byte in C.
;=======================================================================
B6ST:   LD      C,BCONIO
        LD      E,DCIST
        CALL    BDOS
        OR      A
        RET
B6IN:   LD      C,BCONIO
        LD      E,DCIRD
        CALL    BDOS
        OR      A
        JR      Z,B6IN          ; no key yet: ask again
        RET
; B6OUT - console output on CP/M 3.
;
; Direct console I/O reserves three values of E: FFh, FEh and FDh mean
; "read", "status" and "read, waiting" - so passing a DATA byte of FDh or
; above through function 6 does not print it.  It performs an input
; instead, silently dropping the byte and, worse, eating a keystroke the
; user had typed.  A terminal passing 8-bit data through would hit this.
;
; Those three go out through function 2 instead, which has no reserved
; values.  Function 2 expands tabs, which is why it is not used for
; everything - but FDh..FFh are not tabs, so nothing is lost.
B6OUT:  LD      A,C
        CP      0FDH
        JR      NC,B6OUT1
        LD      E,A
        LD      C,BCONIO
        JP      BDOS
B6OUT1: LD      E,A
        LD      C,BCONOU
        JP      BDOS

;=======================================================================
; Console helpers.  COUT takes the byte in A (the rest of the program
; thinks in A; the BIOS wants C).
;
; These preserve BC, DE and HL because what they call does not: a CP/M
; BIOS is free to destroy any register, and the BDOS certainly does.
; Assuming otherwise cost an afternoon - printing a string through an
; unprotected wrapper emitted its first character forever, because the
; console call had left the string pointer pointing at itself.
;
; The cost is 66 T-states on a path that is already waiting on a UART at
; a millisecond per byte, which is the right trade: the terminal loop is
; made cheap by holding no state across calls at all, not by shaving
; pushes off the one routine every screen depends on.
;
; CST also normalises the flags, since neither a BIOS console-status
; routine nor the BDOS promises that Z reflects the value it returns.
;=======================================================================
CST:    PUSH    BC
        PUSH    DE
        PUSH    HL
        CALL    VCST
        POP     HL
        POP     DE
        POP     BC
        OR      A
        RET
CIN:    PUSH    BC
        PUSH    DE
        PUSH    HL
        CALL    VCIN
        POP     HL
        POP     DE
        POP     BC
        RET
COUT:   PUSH    AF
        PUSH    BC
        PUSH    DE
        PUSH    HL
        LD      C,A
        CALL    VCOUT
        POP     HL
        POP     DE
        POP     BC
        POP     AF
        RET

;=======================================================================
; CRLF - end a line.
;=======================================================================
CRLF:   LD      A,CR
        CALL    COUT
        LD      A,LF
        JP      COUT

;=======================================================================
; PSTR - print the zero-terminated string at HL.
;
; Zero-terminated rather than '$'-terminated so that '$' can appear in
; text, and so printing does not depend on the BDOS being reachable.
;=======================================================================
PSTR:   LD      A,(HL)
        OR      A
        RET     Z
        CALL    COUT
        INC     HL
        JR      PSTR

;=======================================================================
; PHEX - print A as two hex digits.  Used for port addresses, which the
; user needs to see in the same form the hardware documentation uses.
;=======================================================================
PHEX:   PUSH    AF
        RRCA
        RRCA
        RRCA
        RRCA
        CALL    PHEXD
        POP     AF
        CALL    PHEXD
        RET
PHEXD:  AND     0FH
        ADD     A,90H
        DAA
        ADC     A,40H
        DAA
        JP      COUT

;=======================================================================
; UPCASE - fold the character in A to upper case, so menus accept either.
;=======================================================================
UPCASE: CP      'a'
        RET     C
        CP      'z'+1
        RET     NC
        SUB     20H
        RET


;=======================================================================
; PORT LAYER
;
; PORTST points the four port vectors at the driver for the selected kind.
; It runs at startup and whenever the user changes the port, so the
; vectors always agree with the settings block.  A driver is reached
; through those vectors, so the terminal loop pays one CALL per byte no
; matter how many families exist.
;
; PBASE means whatever the selected family needs: the status port for
; SIO/2 and the ACIA, the unit number for HBIOS, the internal I/O base
; for the Z180 ASCI (with the channel in PCHAN), and nothing at all for
; the CP/M AUX device.
;=======================================================================
PORTST: CALL    PORTS1
        JP      LSAUTO          ; re-apply the line settings, if any were
                                ; asked for: that is what makes them a
                                ; setting and not a one-off command
PORTS1: LD      A,(PKIND)
        CP      PKSIO
        JR      Z,PSSIO
        CP      PKACIA
        JR      Z,PSACI
        CP      PKHBIO
        JR      Z,PSHB
        CP      PKASCI
        JR      Z,PSASC
        CP      PKAUX
        JR      Z,PSAUX
        LD      HL,PNULL        ; nothing selected: safe stubs
        LD      DE,PNULL
        JR      PSSET4
PSSIO:  LD      HL,SIOST
        LD      DE,SIOIN
        CALL    PSSET2
        LD      HL,SIOOST
        LD      DE,SIOOUT
        JR      PSSETO
PSACI:  CALL    ACINIT          ; a 6850 needs programming before use
        LD      HL,ACIST
        LD      DE,ACIIN
        CALL    PSSET2
        LD      HL,ACIOST
        LD      DE,ACIOUT
        JR      PSSETO
PSHB:   LD      HL,HBST
        LD      DE,HBIN
        CALL    PSSET2
        LD      HL,HBOST
        LD      DE,HBOUT
        JR      PSSETO
PSASC:  CALL    ASCPAT          ; patch the register addresses in place
        LD      HL,ASCST
        LD      DE,ASCIN
        CALL    PSSET2
        LD      HL,ASCOST
        LD      DE,ASCOUT
        JR      PSSETO
PSAUX:  XOR     A
        LD      (AUXFLG),A      ; drop any byte held from a previous port
        LD      HL,AUXST
        LD      DE,AUXIN
        CALL    PSSET2
        LD      HL,AUXOST
        LD      DE,AUXOUT
        JR      PSSETO

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; PSSET2 - point the input pair (status in HL, read in DE) at a driver;
; called twice, the second time for the output pair.  A tiny helper, but
; it turns five nearly identical driver-selection blocks into two lines
; each, which is the difference between adding a family being cheap and
; being a copy-and-paste hazard.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
PSSET2: LD      (VPST+1),HL
        LD      (VPIN+1),DE
        RET
PSSETO: LD      (VPOST+1),HL
        LD      (VPOUT+1),DE
        RET
PSSET4: LD      (VPST+1),HL     ; all four at once, for the null stubs
        LD      (VPIN+1),HL
        LD      (VPOST+1),HL
        LD      (VPOUT+1),HL
        RET

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; PNULL - the no-port-selected stub.  Reports "never ready, never sends"
; rather than reading a port that may not exist: on real hardware an IN
; from an unpopulated address returns floating bus noise, which would
; look like incoming data.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
PNULL:  XOR     A
        RET

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; Z80 SIO/2 driver.  PBASE holds the control/status port; data is the
; next address up, which is how every RC2014-class SIO/2 board is wired.
; RR0: bit 0 = receive character available, bit 2 = transmit buffer
; empty.  B is cleared before IN A,(C) because the Z80 puts BC on the
; address bus and stray high bits confuse partially-decoded boards.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
SIOST:  LD      A,(PBASE)
        LD      C,A
        LD      B,0
        IN      A,(C)
        AND     01H
        RET
SIOIN:  LD      A,(PBASE)
        INC     A
        LD      C,A
        LD      B,0
        IN      A,(C)
        RET
SIOOST: LD      A,(PBASE)
        LD      C,A
        LD      B,0
        IN      A,(C)
        AND     04H
        RET
SIOOUT: LD      E,A             ; hold the byte; A is needed for the port
        LD      A,(PBASE)
        INC     A
        LD      C,A
        LD      B,0
        LD      A,E
        OUT     (C),A
        RET

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; 6850 ACIA driver (Altair 88-2SIO, RC2014 ACIA boards, and many others).
; PBASE holds the control/status port, data the next address up.  Status:
; bit 0 = receive data register full, bit 1 = transmit data register
; empty.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
ACIST:  LD      A,(PBASE)
        LD      C,A
        LD      B,0
        IN      A,(C)
        AND     01H
        RET
ACIIN:  LD      A,(PBASE)
        INC     A
        LD      C,A
        LD      B,0
        IN      A,(C)
        RET
ACIOST: LD      A,(PBASE)
        LD      C,A
        LD      B,0
        IN      A,(C)
        AND     02H
        RET
ACIOUT: LD      E,A
        LD      A,(PBASE)
        INC     A
        LD      C,A
        LD      B,0
        LD      A,E
        OUT     (C),A
        RET

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; ACINIT - a 6850 will not talk until it is programmed, and unlike the
; SIO it forgets nothing gracefully: a master reset (03H) followed by
; 8 data bits, no parity, 1 stop bit at the /16 clock divisor (15H).
; Done when the port is selected rather than at startup, so choosing a
; different family never writes to a chip the user did not pick.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
ACINIT: LD      A,(PBASE)
        LD      C,A
        LD      B,0
        LD      A,03H           ; master reset
        OUT     (C),A
        LD      A,15H           ; 8N1, /16 clock, receive interrupt off
        OUT     (C),A
        RET

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; RomWBW HBIOS driver.  No port at all: the firmware moves the byte.  A
; call is RST 8 with the function in B and the unit in C, and the result
; comes back in A with the flags set from it - which is exactly the
; convention the vectors here want, so the status calls need no masking.
; PBASE holds the unit number.
;
; Only usable on a RomWBW machine (or the gateway's emulator with an
; hbios profile); SCPHB checks for the RST 8 vector before selecting it,
; because on a bare CP/M machine RST 8 lands in unused memory.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
HBST:   LD      B,02H           ; serial input status
        LD      A,(PBASE)
        LD      C,A
        RST     8
        RET
HBIN:   LD      B,00H           ; serial input (waits for a character)
        LD      A,(PBASE)
        LD      C,A
        RST     8
        LD      A,E             ; the byte arrives in E
        RET
HBOST:  LD      B,03H           ; serial output status
        LD      A,(PBASE)
        LD      C,A
        RST     8
        RET
HBOUT:  LD      E,A             ; the byte goes out in E
        LD      B,01H           ; serial output
        LD      A,(PBASE)
        LD      C,A
        RST     8
        RET


;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; HBCNT - how many character units does HBIOS have?  Count in A, carry set
; if there is no HBIOS to ask.
;
; SYSGET (0F8H) sub-function 00H, the published CIOCNT call: count in E.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
HBCNT:  LD      A,(0008H)
        CP      0C3H            ; no RST 8 vector: not a RomWBW machine
        JR      NZ,HBCNTN
        LD      B,0F8H
        LD      C,00H
        RST     8
        OR      A
        JR      NZ,HBCNTN
        LD      A,E             ; LD leaves the flags alone, so the carry
        RET                     ; stays clear from the OR above
HBCNTN: XOR     A
        SCF
        RET

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; HBDEV - ask HBIOS to describe character unit A (CIODEVICE, 06H).
;
; Carry set if there is no HBIOS or it refuses the unit - which is how a
; unit that does not exist answers, so a caller can list what is really
; there instead of offering a fixed 0-3.  On success HBTYPE holds the
; device type, and HBBASE / HBPHYS the base I/O address and physical
; channel the firmware reports.
;
; The unit number travels through memory rather than a register because
; RST 8 is someone else's code: nothing but the documented return
; registers can be assumed to survive it.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
HBDEV:  LD      (HBUNIT),A
        LD      A,(0008H)
        CP      0C3H
        JR      NZ,HBDEVN
        LD      A,(HBUNIT)
        LD      C,A
        LD      B,06H
        RST     8
        OR      A
        JR      NZ,HBDEVN
        LD      A,D
        LD      (HBTYPE),A
        LD      A,E
        LD      (HBPHYS),A
        LD      A,L
        LD      (HBBASE),A
        OR      A               ; clears carry whatever A holds
        RET
HBDEVN: SCF
        RET

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; SHTYPE - print the device-type name HBIOS reported, from the published
; list of character-device drivers.  An unknown type prints as a hex
; number rather than a guess - the gateway's own virtual modem is one,
; being no real chip at all.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
SHTYPE: LD      A,(HBTYPE)
        CP      HBTMAX
        JR      NC,SHTYPU
        LD      E,A             ; five bytes per name: A*4 + A
        ADD     A,A
        ADD     A,A
        ADD     A,E
        LD      E,A
        LD      D,0
        LD      HL,HBTNAM
        ADD     HL,DE
        LD      B,5
SHTYP1: LD      A,(HL)
        CALL    COUT
        INC     HL
        DEC     B
        JR      NZ,SHTYP1
        RET
SHTYPU: LD      A,'?'
        CALL    COUT
        LD      A,(HBTYPE)
        CALL    PHEX
        LD      A,' '
        CALL    COUT
        LD      A,' '
        JP      COUT

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; Z180 ASCI driver (SC126 and other Z180 boards).
;
; The ASCI registers are Z180 *internal* I/O, reached with IN0/OUT0 -
; instructions a Z80 assembler cannot encode, so they are laid down as
; DB ED,38,port (IN0 A,(port)) and DB ED,39,port (OUT0 (port),A).  The
; operand is an immediate byte, so ASCPAT writes the addresses into these
; instructions when the port is selected: the same self-patching the
; vectors use, one level down.  Every such instruction is labelled so the
; patcher names what it is writing to rather than counting offsets.
;
; Register layout, relative to the internal I/O base (PABASE, 00H unless
; the system has moved it) - channel 1 is the same +1:
;
;   CNTLA0 = base+0    STAT0 = base+4    TDR0 = base+6    RDR0 = base+8
;
; STAT: bit 7 RDRF, bit 6 OVRN, bit 5 PE, bit 4 FE, bit 1 TDRE.
; CNTLA: bit 3 EFR - writing a 0 there resets the three error flags.
;
; Baud rate and framing are left alone by the *driver*: they live in CNTLA
; and CNTLB, the system set them, and a terminal has no business re-timing
; a port that is already working.  The only write to CNTLA here is the
; error reset, and it is read-modify-write so nothing else changes.  Line
; settings can be programmed deliberately from the Settings screen
; (LSASCI), which is a different thing from a driver doing it behind the
; user's back.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
ASCST:  DB      0EDH,38H
ASCS1:  DB      00H             ; IN0 A,(STATn)  - operand patched
        LD      E,A
        AND     70H             ; OVRN, PE or FE latched?
        JR      NZ,ASCERR
        LD      A,E
        AND     80H             ; RDRF
        RET
ASCIN:  DB      0EDH,38H
ASCR1:  DB      00H             ; IN0 A,(RDRn)
        RET
ASCOST: DB      0EDH,38H
ASCS2:  DB      00H             ; IN0 A,(STATn)
        AND     02H             ; TDRE
        RET
ASCOUT: DB      0EDH,39H
ASCT1:  DB      00H             ; OUT0 (TDRn),A
        RET

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; ASCERR - a receive error is latched.  This matters more than it looks:
; on the Z180 an overrun stops the receiver until the error is reset, so
; a single burst of line noise would otherwise leave the port deaf for
; the rest of the session with no indication of why.
;
; The byte that arrived with the error is discarded rather than handed up
; - it is known bad, and a protocol is better served by a missing byte it
; will ask for again than by a corrupt one it has to detect.  Then the
; flags are reset and "nothing to read" is reported.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
ASCERR: LD      A,E
        AND     80H             ; did a byte arrive with the error?
        JR      Z,ASCER1
        DB      0EDH,38H
ASCR2:  DB      00H             ; IN0 A,(RDRn) - discard the suspect byte
ASCER1: DB      0EDH,38H
ASCC1:  DB      00H             ; IN0 A,(CNTLAn)
        AND     0F7H            ; EFR = 0 resets OVRN/PE/FE
        DB      0EDH,39H
ASCC2:  DB      00H             ; OUT0 (CNTLAn),A
        XOR     A               ; report: nothing readable this time
        RET

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; ASCPAT - write the register addresses into the ASCI routines.  Every
; patched operand in the family belongs here, the four line-settings ones
; included; a routine that patches only its own is a routine that works
; until someone selects channel 1.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
ASCPAT: LD      A,(PCHAN)
        AND     01H             ; channel 0 or 1
        LD      E,A
        LD      A,(PABASE)      ; internal I/O base (00H unless moved)
        ADD     A,E
        LD      D,A             ; D = base + channel
        LD      A,D
        ADD     A,04H           ; STATn
        LD      (ASCS1),A
        LD      (ASCS2),A
        LD      A,D
        ADD     A,08H           ; RDRn
        LD      (ASCR1),A
        LD      (ASCR2),A
        LD      A,D
        ADD     A,06H           ; TDRn
        LD      (ASCT1),A
        LD      A,D             ; CNTLAn is the base itself
        LD      (ASCC1),A
        LD      (ASCC2),A
        LD      (LSASA1),A      ; ...and the line-settings pair, which must be
        LD      (LSASA2),A      ; patched here too: they are the same kind of
        LD      A,D             ; self-modified operand, and left at 00H they
        ADD     A,02H           ; would address channel 0 of an unmoved I/O
        LD      (LSASB1),A      ; base whatever the user picked - writing
        LD      (LSASB2),A      ; CNTLB's value into CNTLA, whose RE and TE
        RET                     ; bits would then silence the port

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; ISZ180 - is this processor a Z180?  Carry set if it is not.
;
; The ASCI lives inside the CPU, so selecting it on a plain Z80 does not
; just fail - IN0/OUT0 are undefined there, and the driver would read
; whatever an undefined opcode leaves behind.  Better to know first.
;
; MLT is the convenient test: on a Z180, ED 4C multiplies B by C into BC;
; on a Z80 that opcode is undefined and behaves as a two-byte no-op, so
; BC comes back untouched.  2 x 2 = 4 separates the two with no side
; effects on either.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
ISZ180: LD      BC,0202H
        DB      0EDH,4CH        ; MLT BC on a Z180; no-op on a Z80
        LD      A,B
        OR      A
        JR      NZ,ISZNOT       ; B still 2: not a Z180
        LD      A,C
        CP      04H             ; 2 x 2
        JR      NZ,ISZNOT
        XOR     A               ; carry clear: this is a Z180
        RET
ISZNOT: SCF
        RET

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; CP/M AUX device driver (BDOS functions 3 and 4).
;
; The portable fallback: no hardware knowledge at all, so it works
; wherever the operating system has an AUX device - including the
; gateway's emulator, where AUX is wired to the virtual modem.
;
; CP/M 2.2 has NO way to ask whether an AUX byte is waiting, which is the
; catch.  The status call therefore READS one byte and holds it: the
; gateway returns ^Z when nothing is waiting, so ^Z means "not ready" and
; anything else is stashed for the next read.  That makes polling work
; here at the price of not being able to receive a literal ^Z on this
; family, and on real CP/M 2.2 hardware - where BDOS 3 blocks until a
; byte arrives - the status call waits rather than returning.  Both are
; consequences of the missing status call, not choices; use a hardware
; family instead when one fits the machine.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
AUXST:  LD      A,(AUXFLG)      ; already holding one?
        OR      A
        RET     NZ
        PUSH    BC
        PUSH    DE
        PUSH    HL
        LD      C,03H           ; BDOS 3: AUX input
        CALL    BDOS
        POP     HL
        POP     DE
        POP     BC
        CP      1AH             ; ^Z = nothing waiting
        JR      Z,AUXST0
        LD      (AUXHLD),A
        LD      A,1
        LD      (AUXFLG),A
        RET                     ; A is non-zero: ready
AUXST0: XOR     A
        RET
AUXIN:  CALL    AUXST           ; make sure a byte is held
        XOR     A
        LD      (AUXFLG),A
        LD      A,(AUXHLD)
        RET
AUXOST: LD      A,1             ; the BDOS blocks for us: always ready
        RET
AUXOUT: PUSH    BC
        PUSH    DE
        PUSH    HL
        LD      E,A
        LD      C,04H           ; BDOS 4: AUX output
        CALL    BDOS
        POP     HL
        POP     DE
        POP     BC
        RET

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; Port vector call sites.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
PST:    JP      VPST
PIN:    JP      VPIN
POST:   JP      VPOST
POUT:   JP      VPOUT

;=======================================================================
; PSEND - send the byte in A out the port, waiting for room.
;
; The wait is BOUNDED, and that matters more than it sounds.  If the user
; has picked the wrong port, the transmitter is never ready - an absent
; chip answers every read the same way forever - so an unbounded wait
; hangs the program at the first keystroke, with the menu key dead
; because the loop never gets back to reading the keyboard.  That is the
; exact moment a user needs the menu, so instead the send gives up, sets
; PDEAD, and terminal mode reports what happened and hands back control.
;
; The bound is deliberately generous - four passes of a 65536-poll loop,
; several seconds on a 4 MHz Z80 - because a *working* port can legally
; stall for a long time when hardware flow control is holding it off.
; Diagnosing a dead port must not misfire on a slow peer.
;
; The counter lives in BC and is saved around the status call, because
; the port drivers use BC for the I/O address.
;=======================================================================
PSEND:  PUSH    BC              ; BC is this routine's poll counter; callers
        LD      (SENDCH),A      ; count block bytes in B, so hand it back
        LD      A,4
        LD      (PTRIES),A
PSNDO:  LD      BC,0            ; 65536 polls per pass
PSEND1: PUSH    BC
        CALL    POST
        POP     BC
        JR      NZ,PSEND2
        DEC     BC
        LD      A,B
        OR      C
        JR      NZ,PSEND1
        LD      A,(PTRIES)
        DEC     A
        LD      (PTRIES),A
        JR      NZ,PSNDO
        POP     BC
        LD      A,1
        LD      (PDEAD),A       ; transmitter never came free
        RET
PSEND2: LD      A,(SENDCH)
        CALL    POUT            ; the driver clobbers BC, so restore AFTER it
        POP     BC
        RET

;=======================================================================
; MAIN MENU
;=======================================================================
MAINLP: CALL    SCMENU
        CALL    CIN
        CALL    UPCASE
        CP      'T'
        JR      Z,DOTERM
        CP      'S'
        JR      Z,DOSETT
        CP      'U'
        JR      Z,DOSEND
        CP      'D'
        JR      Z,DORECV
        CP      'H'
        JR      Z,DOHELP
        CP      'V'
        JR      Z,DOSAVE
        CP      'X'
        JR      Z,DOEXIT
        LD      A,BEL           ; unknown key: a beep, no scolding
        CALL    COUT
        JR      MAINLP
DOTERM: CALL    TERM
        JR      MAINLP
DOSETT: CALL    SCSETT
        JR      MAINLP
DOHELP: CALL    SCHELP
        JR      MAINLP
DOSAVE: CALL    SAVCFG
        CALL    TWAIT           ; the menu redraw clears the screen, and
        JR      MAINLP          ; whether it saved is worth reading
DOSEND: CALL    XSEND
        CALL    TWAIT
        JR      MAINLP
DORECV: CALL    XRECV
        CALL    TWAIT
        JR      MAINLP
DOEXIT: CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MBYE
        CALL    PSTR
        JP      WBOOT

;=======================================================================
; TERMINAL MODE
;
; The hot loop.  Port first, then console: incoming data is the side that
; loses bytes if it is left waiting, while a human's keystroke can spare
; the microseconds.
;=======================================================================
TERM:   LD      A,(PKIND)
        OR      A
        JR      NZ,TERM1
        LD      HL,MNOPRT       ; refuse rather than read a dead port
        CALL    PSTR
        JP      TCWAIT          ; ...and they may already be typing
TERM1:  CALL    SCTTL           ; the banner heads every session, so it is
        CALL    CRLF            ; always clear which program you are talking
        LD      HL,MTERM        ; to once the remote end fills the screen
        CALL    PSTR
        CALL    SHESC           ; name the menu key - a terminal you cannot
        LD      HL,MTERM2       ; get out of is the worst kind
        CALL    PSTR
        CALL    SHESC
        LD      HL,MTERM3
        CALL    PSTR
        XOR     A
        LD      (FSTATE),A      ; no half-finished escape sequence
        LD      (ESTATE),A      ; on either stream
        LD      (PDEAD),A       ; and no diagnosis pending
TLOOP:  CALL    PST             ; anything from the port?
        JR      Z,TL1
        CALL    PIN
        CALL    FILT            ; filter, then to the console
TL1:    CALL    CST             ; anything from the keyboard?
        JR      Z,TLOOP
        CALL    CIN
        LD      HL,ESCCH
        CP      (HL)            ; the escape key?
        JR      Z,TESC
        CALL    TSEND
        LD      A,(PDEAD)
        OR      A
        JR      NZ,TDEAD
        JP      TLOOP

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; TDEAD - the port never accepted the byte.  Name the port, say what it
; means, and leave terminal mode so the menu is reachable again.  This is
; the failure every other terminal here presents as a silent hang.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
TDEAD:  CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MDEAD
        CALL    PSTR
        CALL    SHPORT
        LD      HL,MDEAD2
        CALL    PSTR
        JP      TCWAIT          ; the user is mid-sentence: only ^C leaves

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; TSEND - send a typed character, honouring local echo and CR handling.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
TSEND:  LD      (TYPECH),A
        LD      A,(ECHOFL)
        OR      A
        JR      Z,TSEND1
        LD      A,(TYPECH)
        CALL    FILTE           ; echo through its own filter state
TSEND1: LD      A,(TYPECH)
        CALL    PSEND
        LD      A,(TYPECH)
        CP      CR
        RET     NZ
        LD      A,(CRLFFL)      ; append LF to a typed CR?
        OR      A
        RET     Z
        LD      A,(ECHOFL)      ; if the echo is ours, echo the LF too -
        OR      A               ; otherwise the local screen keeps
        JR      Z,TSEND2        ; overwriting the same line
        LD      A,LF
        CALL    FILTE
TSEND2: LD      A,LF
        JP      PSEND

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; TESC - the escape key was pressed in terminal mode.
;
; One keystroke decides: leave, get help, or send the escape character
; itself (pressing it twice), so the key is never trapped away from the
; remote end.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
TESC:   CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MESCMN
        CALL    PSTR
        CALL    CIN
        CALL    UPCASE
        LD      HL,ESCCH        ; escape twice: send it literally
        CP      (HL)
        JR      Z,TESCLI
        CP      'E'
        JR      Z,TESCEX
        CP      'H'
        JR      Z,TESCHL
        CP      'S'
        JR      Z,TESCST
        CP      'U'
        JR      Z,TESCU
        CP      'D'
        JR      Z,TESCD
        CALL    CRLF            ; anything else: back to the session
        JP      TLOOP
TESCLI: LD      A,(ESCCH)
        CALL    PSEND
        JP      TLOOP
TESCHL: CALL    SCHELP
        JP      TLOOP
TESCST: CALL    SCSETT
        JP      TLOOP
TESCU:  CALL    XSEND           ; transfer without leaving the session
        CALL    TWAIT
        JP      TLOOP
TESCD:  CALL    XRECV
        CALL    TWAIT
        JP      TLOOP
TESCEX: CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MTEXIT
        CALL    PSTR
        RET

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; TCWAIT - wait for Ctrl-C, swallowing everything else.
;
; For a message that appears while the user is TYPING rather than reading.
; A wrong port stops the very first keystroke, but the rest of the
; sentence is already on its way: with a "press any key" pause, one of
; those characters dismisses the notice and the remainder land in the main
; menu as commands - so the user's half-typed sentence gets *executed*.
;
; Only ^C leaves.  Every other byte is read and dropped, so a user who
; keeps typing sees the notice stay put instead of watching their words
; run a menu.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
TCWAIT: LD      HL,MCTRLC
        CALL    PSTR
TCWAI1: CALL    CIN
        CP      03H             ; ^C
        JR      NZ,TCWAI1       ; anything else: swallow it
        CALL    CRLF
        RET

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; TWAIT - "press a key" pause, for screens the user chose to read
; (help, a finished transfer) where a stray keystroke costs nothing.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
TWAIT:  LD      HL,MKEY
        CALL    PSTR
        CALL    CIN
        CALL    CRLF
        RET

;=======================================================================
; FILT / FILTE - filter a byte to the console.  Byte in A.
;
; FILT  is the INBOUND path (bytes from the port).
; FILTE is the LOCAL ECHO path (bytes the user just typed).
;
; They are the same code over SEPARATE state bytes, and that separation
; matters: the state machine spans several bytes of an escape sequence, so
; a single shared state would let a typed ESC swallow the next byte that
; arrived from the port - two independent streams cannot share one
; half-finished sequence.  HL carries the state byte's address through the
; whole routine, which is why nothing here may clobber it.
;
; ANSI mode passes everything through, for a terminal that understands
; escape sequences.  ASCII mode is for a printing terminal or a console
; with no cursor addressing: the high bit is stripped (the classic
; parity-bit strip, so 8-bit text reads as ASCII rather than as garbage),
; CSI sequences are swallowed whole, DEL is dropped, and of the control
; codes only CR, LF, TAB, BS and BEL - the ones that do useful work on any
; terminal - are let through.
;
; State: 0 = normal, 1 = an ESC was seen, 2 = inside a CSI sequence.  The MODE
; is tested before the state, and ANSI clears it: otherwise switching from
; ASCII to ANSI part-way through a sequence would keep swallowing bytes on the
; new setting until that sequence happened to finish.
;=======================================================================
FILT:   LD      HL,FSTATE       ; inbound stream's own state
        JR      FILT0
FILTE:  LD      HL,ESTATE       ; echo stream's own state
FILT0:  LD      E,A             ; hold the byte
        LD      A,(FLTMOD)
        OR      A
        JR      NZ,FASCII
        LD      (HL),0          ; ANSI: no sequence state to carry
        LD      A,E
        JP      COUT            ; straight through
FASCII: LD      A,(HL)          ; ASCII: mid-sequence?
        OR      A
        JR      NZ,FILT2
        LD      A,E
        AND     7FH             ; strip the high bit
        LD      E,A
        CP      ESC
        JR      Z,FSESC
        CP      DEL
        RET     Z               ; DEL prints as litter: drop it
        CP      20H
        JR      NC,FPRIN        ; printable
        CP      CR
        JR      Z,FPRIN
        CP      LF
        JR      Z,FPRIN
        CP      TAB
        JR      Z,FPRIN
        CP      BS
        JR      Z,FPRIN
        CP      BEL
        JR      Z,FPRIN
        RET                     ; other control code: drop it
FPRIN:  JP      COUT
FSESC:  LD      (HL),1
        RET

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; FILT2 - mid-sequence.  After ESC, '[' opens a CSI (swallow until its
; final byte, 40H-7EH); anything else is a two-character sequence, so it
; and the ESC are both gone.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
FILT2:  DEC     A
        JR      NZ,FCSI         ; state 2: already inside a CSI
        LD      A,E
        AND     7FH
        CP      '['
        JR      Z,FCSIST
        LD      (HL),0          ; two-character escape: done
        RET
FCSIST: LD      (HL),2
        RET
FCSI:   LD      A,E
        AND     7FH
        CP      40H
        RET     C               ; parameter byte: keep swallowing
        CP      7FH
        RET     NC
        LD      (HL),0          ; final byte: sequence complete
        RET



;=======================================================================
; XMODEM
;
; Plain XMODEM: 128-byte blocks, CRC-16 with the checksum fallback a
; vintage peer may insist on.  One 128-byte buffer serves as both the
; protocol block and the CP/M record, because they are the same size -
; that is why XMODEM and CP/M fit together so neatly, and it is what
; keeps the memory cost of transfers to a single buffer.
;
; The CRC is computed a bit at a time rather than from a 512-byte table.
; A table would cost more memory than the whole transfer code and buy
; nothing: at 9600 baud a byte takes a millisecond and the CRC of one
; takes microseconds, so the wire is the limit, not the arithmetic.
;
; Both directions run from the menu with the connection already up, so
; the usual sequence is: dial in terminal mode, tell the far end what you
; want, then press U or D here.  Leaving terminal mode does not hang up.
;=======================================================================

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; XSEND - send a file (upload to the far end).
;
; The receiver drives the start: it sends 'C' for CRC mode or NAK for
; checksum, repeatedly, and we answer whichever arrives.  That is the
; protocol's one piece of negotiation, and honouring it is what makes a
; transfer work against both a modern peer and a 1980s one.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
XSEND:  CALL    XREADY          ; is a port selected at all?
        RET     C
        CALL    RSTDMA          ; start from CP/M's own record buffer
        LD      HL,MXSFIL
        CALL    PSTR
        CALL    XFNAME
        RET     C
        LD      DE,XFCB         ; must exist to be sent
        LD      C,BOPEN
        CALL    BDOS
        INC     A
        JR      NZ,XSEND1
        LD      HL,MXNOFIL
        CALL    PSTR
        RET
XSEND1: LD      A,XNOISE
        LD      (XNCNT),A
        LD      HL,MXWAIT
        CALL    PSTR
        ; No XPURGE here on purpose.  The receiver is already sending its
        ; 'C' every few seconds, and a purge window on a slow machine can
        ; be long enough to swallow each one as it arrives - the sender
        ; would then wait out the whole negotiation with the answer in its
        ; hand.  The handshake loop below tolerates noise instead.
        LD      A,XRETRY
        LD      (XCNT),A
XSEND2: CALL    XCHKAB          ; waiting for the far end is the likeliest
        JP      C,XUSER         ; moment to change your mind
        LD      A,XLONG         ; wait for the receiver to ask
        LD      (XTMO),A
        CALL    XGETB
        JR      NC,XSEND3
        LD      A,(XCNT)        ; nothing yet: keep waiting a while
        DEC     A
        LD      (XCNT),A
        JR      NZ,XSEND2
        JP      XFAIL
XSEND3: CP      CRCC
        JR      Z,XSENDC
        CP      NAK
        JR      Z,XSENDK
        CP      CAN
        JP      Z,XABORT
        ; Noise.  Not an error: the far end usually prints something like
        ; "start your transfer now" first, and every character of it
        ; arrives here.  So it gets its own generous budget, kept apart
        ; from the retry count - spending a retry per character of a
        ; banner would fail the transfer before it began, which is
        ; exactly what an earlier version of this did.
        LD      A,(XNCNT)
        DEC     A
        LD      (XNCNT),A
        JR      NZ,XSEND2
        JP      XFAIL
XSENDC: LD      A,1             ; CRC mode
        JR      XSEND4
XSENDK: XOR     A               ; checksum mode
XSEND4: LD      (XCRCM),A
        CALL    XPURGE          ; drop the far end's "start now" banner: from
                                ; here on, anything arriving is protocol
        LD      A,1
        LD      (XBLK),A        ; XMODEM blocks start at 1
        LD      HL,MXGO
        CALL    PSTR

;--- the per-record loop: read one CP/M record, send it, wait for ACK ---
XSLOOP: CALL    XCHKAB          ; a keypress can stop this
        JP      C,XUSER
        LD      DE,XBUF
        LD      C,BSETDMA
        CALL    BDOS
        LD      DE,XFCB
        LD      C,BREADS
        CALL    BDOS
        OR      A
        JR      NZ,XSDONE       ; no more records: end of file
        LD      A,XRETRY
        LD      (XCNT),A
XSBLK:  CALL    XBLKSN          ; put the block on the wire
XSWAIT: LD      A,XLONG
        LD      (XTMO),A
        CALL    XGETB
        JR      C,XSBAD         ; silence: send the block again
        CP      ACK
        JR      Z,XSNEXT
        CP      CAN
        JP      Z,XABORT
        CP      NAK
        JR      Z,XSBAD         ; asked for it again
        LD      A,(XNCNT)       ; anything else is noise: keep waiting for a
        DEC     A               ; real answer instead of resending on it
        LD      (XNCNT),A
        JR      NZ,XSWAIT
        JP      XFAIL
XSBAD:  LD      A,'?'           ; resend this block
        CALL    COUT
        LD      A,(XCNT)
        DEC     A
        LD      (XCNT),A
        JR      NZ,XSBLK
        JP      XFAIL
XSNEXT: LD      A,'.'
        CALL    COUT
        LD      HL,XBLK
        INC     (HL)            ; block numbers wrap at 256, as they must
        JR      XSLOOP

;--- end of file: EOT until acknowledged, then tidy up ---
XSDONE: LD      A,XRETRY
        LD      (XCNT),A
XSEOT:  LD      A,EOT
        CALL    PSEND
        LD      A,XLONG
        LD      (XTMO),A
        CALL    XGETB
        JR      C,XSEOT1
        CP      ACK
        JR      Z,XSOK
XSEOT1: LD      A,(XCNT)
        DEC     A
        LD      (XCNT),A
        JR      NZ,XSEOT
        JP      XFAIL
XSOK:   LD      DE,XFCB
        LD      C,BCLOSE
        CALL    BDOS
        CALL    RSTDMA
        CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MXSENT
        CALL    PSTR
        RET

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; XBLKSN - send the block in XBUF: header, 128 bytes, then the check.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
XBLKSN: LD      A,SOH
        CALL    PSEND
        LD      A,(XBLK)
        CALL    PSEND
        LD      A,(XBLK)
        CPL                     ; the header carries the block number twice,
        CALL    PSEND           ; the second time inverted
        LD      HL,0            ; CRC accumulator
        LD      (XCRC),HL
        XOR     A
        LD      (XSUM),A        ; checksum accumulator
        LD      HL,XBUF
        LD      B,128
XBLKS1: LD      A,(HL)
        CALL    PSEND
        LD      A,(HL)
        CALL    XACCUM          ; fold the byte into both checks
        INC     HL
        DEC     B
        JR      NZ,XBLKS1
        LD      A,(XCRCM)
        OR      A
        JR      Z,XBLKS2
        LD      HL,(XCRC)       ; CRC-16, high byte first
        LD      A,H
        CALL    PSEND
        LD      A,L
        JP      PSEND
XBLKS2: LD      A,(XSUM)
        JP      PSEND

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; XRECV - receive a file (download from the far end).
;
; We drive the start here: 'C' every couple of seconds to ask for CRC
; mode, and if the far end never answers, NAK for the rest of the
; attempts - a peer too old to know CRC mode responds to that instead.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
XRECV:  CALL    XREADY
        RET     C
        CALL    RSTDMA          ; the directory search below uses the DMA
        LD      HL,MXRFIL
        CALL    PSTR
        CALL    XFNAME
        RET     C
        LD      DE,XFCB         ; refuse to clobber silently
        LD      C,BSEARF
        CALL    BDOS
        INC     A
        JR      Z,XRECV1
        LD      HL,MXEXIST
        CALL    PSTR
        CALL    CIN
        CALL    UPCASE
        CP      'Y'
        JR      Z,XRECVD
        CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MXKEEP
        CALL    PSTR
        RET
XRECVD: LD      DE,XFCB
        LD      C,BDELET
        CALL    BDOS
XRECV1: LD      DE,XFCB
        LD      C,BMAKE
        CALL    BDOS
        INC     A
        JR      NZ,XRECV2
        LD      HL,MXNOMK       ; directory full, or a bad name
        CALL    PSTR
        RET
XRECV2: LD      A,1
        LD      (XRXACT),A      ; from here a partial file exists on disk
        LD      A,XNOISE
        LD      (XNCNT),A
        LD      A,1
        LD      (XCRCM),A       ; ask for CRC mode first
        LD      A,1
        LD      (XBLK),A
        LD      HL,MXGO
        CALL    PSTR
        CALL    XPURGE
        LD      A,XRETRY+XRETRY ; 'C' attempts, then NAK attempts
        LD      (XCNT),A

;--- ask for a block, then take whatever arrives ---
XRLOOP: LD      A,(XCRCM)
        OR      A
        LD      A,CRCC
        JR      NZ,XRL1
        LD      A,NAK
XRL1:   CALL    PSEND
XRWAIT: CALL    XCHKAB
        JP      C,XUSER
        LD      A,XLONG
        LD      (XTMO),A
        CALL    XGETB
        JR      NC,XRGOT
        LD      A,(XCNT)        ; silence: ask again, and after half the
        DEC     A               ; attempts stop asking for CRC mode
        LD      (XCNT),A
        JP      Z,XFAIL
        CP      XRETRY
        JR      NZ,XRLOOP
        XOR     A
        LD      (XCRCM),A       ; fall back to checksum
        JR      XRLOOP
XRGOT:  CP      SOH
        JR      Z,XRBLK
        CP      EOT
        JP      Z,XRDONE
        CP      CAN
        JP      Z,XABORT
        LD      A,(XNCNT)       ; noise: same generous, separate budget
        DEC     A
        LD      (XNCNT),A
        JP      Z,XFAIL
        JP      XRWAIT

;--- a block header arrived: read it, check it, write it ---
XRBLK:  LD      A,XSHORT        ; the rest of a block must arrive promptly
        LD      (XTMO),A
        CALL    XGETB
        JP      C,XRBAD
        LD      (XGOTBL),A      ; block number as sent
        CALL    XGETB
        JP      C,XRBAD
        LD      HL,XGOTBL       ; ...and its complement must agree
        CPL
        CP      (HL)
        JP      NZ,XRBAD
        LD      HL,0
        LD      (XCRC),HL
        XOR     A
        LD      (XSUM),A
        LD      HL,XBUF
        LD      B,128
XRB1:   CALL    XGETB
        JR      C,XRBAD
        LD      (HL),A
        CALL    XACCUM
        INC     HL
        DEC     B
        JR      NZ,XRB1
        LD      A,(XCRCM)
        OR      A
        JR      Z,XRB2
        CALL    XGETB           ; CRC-16: two bytes, high first
        JR      C,XRBAD
        LD      HL,(XCRC)
        CP      H
        JR      NZ,XRBAD
        CALL    XGETB
        JR      C,XRBAD
        LD      HL,(XCRC)
        CP      L
        JR      NZ,XRBAD
        JR      XRGOOD
XRB2:   CALL    XGETB
        JR      C,XRBAD
        LD      HL,XSUM
        CP      (HL)
        JR      NZ,XRBAD

;--- the block is sound: is it the one we expect? ---
XRGOOD: LD      HL,XBLK
        LD      A,(XGOTBL)
        CP      (HL)
        JR      Z,XRSAVE
        ; Not the expected block.  If it is the PREVIOUS one, the sender
        ; never saw our ACK and has resent it: acknowledge again and do
        ; NOT write it a second time.  Anything else means the two ends
        ; have lost step, which is a bad block.
        ;
        ; Note the register work: the comparison result has to survive
        ; until the branch, so "expected minus one" is computed in A
        ; rather than by DEC/CP/INC on the counter in memory - INC (HL)
        ; sets the flags itself and would overwrite the very answer the
        ; branch is waiting for.
        LD      A,(HL)
        DEC     A               ; the block before the one we want
        LD      HL,XGOTBL
        CP      (HL)
        JR      NZ,XRBAD        ; a different block entirely: out of step
        LD      A,ACK
        CALL    PSEND
        JP      XRRSET
XRSAVE: LD      DE,XBUF
        LD      C,BSETDMA
        CALL    BDOS
        LD      DE,XFCB
        LD      C,BWRITS
        CALL    BDOS
        OR      A
        JR      NZ,XRDISK       ; disk full: stop rather than lose data
        LD      A,ACK
        CALL    PSEND
        LD      A,'.'
        CALL    COUT
        LD      HL,XBLK
        INC     (HL)
XRRSET: LD      A,XRETRY        ; progress: a fresh budget of retries
        LD      (XCNT),A
        LD      A,XNOISE        ; ...and of tolerance for junk
        LD      (XNCNT),A
        JP      XRWAIT

;--- a bad block: drain the line, NAK, try again ---
XRBAD:  LD      A,'?'
        CALL    COUT
        CALL    XPURGE
        LD      A,(XCNT)
        DEC     A
        LD      (XCNT),A
        JP      Z,XFAIL
        LD      A,NAK
        CALL    PSEND
        JP      XRWAIT

;--- EOT: acknowledge, close, done ---
XRDONE: LD      A,ACK
        CALL    PSEND
        XOR     A
        LD      (XRXACT),A      ; complete: it is a real file now
        LD      DE,XFCB
        LD      C,BCLOSE
        CALL    BDOS
        CALL    RSTDMA
        CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MXRECVD
        CALL    PSTR
        RET

XRDISK: CALL    XCANC
        CALL    RSTDMA
        CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MXDISK
        CALL    PSTR
        JP      XSCRAP           ; deleting it also gives the space back

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; XACCUM - fold the byte in A into both the CRC and the checksum, so the
; block loops need not care which check mode is in force.
;
; CRC-16 with polynomial 1021H, one bit at a time: the byte goes into the
; high half of the accumulator, then eight shifts, XORing the polynomial
; back in whenever a 1 falls off the top.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
XACCUM: PUSH    BC              ; the block loops count 128 bytes in B and
        PUSH    HL              ; walk the buffer with HL; this routine needs
        LD      E,A             ; both, so it borrows and gives them back
        LD      HL,XSUM
        ADD     A,(HL)
        LD      (HL),A          ; checksum: a plain 8-bit sum
        LD      HL,(XCRC)
        LD      A,H
        XOR     E
        LD      H,A
        LD      B,8
XACC1:  ADD     HL,HL
        JR      NC,XACC2
        LD      A,H
        XOR     10H
        LD      H,A
        LD      A,L
        XOR     21H
        LD      L,A
XACC2:  DEC     B
        JR      NZ,XACC1
        LD      (XCRC),HL
        POP     HL
        POP     BC
        LD      A,E
        RET

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; XGETB - wait for a byte from the port.  Byte in A, carry clear; carry
; set if nothing came within (XTMO) passes of a 65536-poll loop.
;
; A poll loop is the only clock a CP/M program can count on - there is no
; timer service in CP/M 2.2 - so the wait is measured in polls, which
; makes it faster on a faster machine.  That is acceptable here: both
; bounds are generous multiples of the time a byte actually takes, and the
; retry counts do the real work of bounding a transfer.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
XGETB:  PUSH    BC              ; BC is this routine's poll counter, and the
        LD      A,(XTMO)        ; block loops are counting in B - hand it back
        LD      (XTMOC),A
XGETB0: LD      BC,0
XGETB1: PUSH    BC
        CALL    PST
        POP     BC
        JR      NZ,XGETB2
        DEC     BC
        LD      A,B
        OR      C
        JR      NZ,XGETB1
        LD      A,(XTMOC)
        DEC     A
        LD      (XTMOC),A
        JR      NZ,XGETB0
        POP     BC
        SCF                     ; nothing arrived
        RET
XGETB2: CALL    PIN
        POP     BC
        OR      A               ; carry clear: a byte is in A
        RET

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; XPURGE - swallow whatever is still coming until the line goes quiet,
; so a retry starts from a known state instead of half-way through the
; block that just failed.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
XPURGE: LD      A,XSHORT
        LD      (XTMO),A
        CALL    XGETB
        JR      NC,XPURGE
        RET

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; XREADY - refuse a transfer when no port is selected, with the same
; wording the terminal uses, rather than "transferring" into nothing.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
XREADY: LD      A,(PKIND)
        OR      A
        JR      Z,XREADY1
        XOR     A               ; a fresh diagnosis for this transfer: the
        LD      (PDEAD),A       ; flag may be left over from a past session
        RET                     ; carry clear: good to go
XREADY1: CALL   CRLF
        LD      HL,MNOPRT
        CALL    PSTR
        SCF
        RET

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; XCANC / XABORT / XFAIL - the ways a transfer ends badly.  Two CANs are
; how XMODEM says "stop"; one can be line noise, which is why the
; protocol asks for two and why we send two.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
XCANC:  LD      A,CAN
        CALL    PSEND
        LD      A,CAN
        JP      PSEND

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; XSCRAP - throw away a partly received file.
;
; A download that stops early has written real records to a real file:
; the directory entry exists and its contents are a prefix of the truth.
; Leaving that on disk is the worst outcome, because it looks like a
; file - the same silent-truncation trap the gateway's own Kermit
; receiver refuses.  So an incomplete download is deleted, and the user
; is told.  XRXACT marks the window in which an output file exists;
; nothing is deleted outside it, so a failed *upload* never touches the
; file it was reading.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
XSCRAP: LD      A,(XRXACT)
        OR      A
        RET     Z               ; no download in progress: nothing to undo
        XOR     A
        LD      (XRXACT),A
        LD      DE,XFCB
        LD      C,BDELET
        CALL    BDOS
        LD      HL,MXSCRAP
        JP      PSTR
XABORT: CALL    RSTDMA
        CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MXCAN
        CALL    PSTR
        JP      XSCRAP
XFAIL:  CALL    XCANC
        CALL    RSTDMA
        CALL    CRLF
        LD      A,(PDEAD)       ; did the PORT stop taking characters?
        OR      A
        JR      NZ,XFAILP
        LD      HL,MXFAIL
        CALL    PSTR
        JP      XSCRAP
XFAILP: LD      HL,MDEAD        ; then give the port diagnosis, not a vague
        CALL    PSTR            ; "the other end stopped answering"
        CALL    SHPORT
        LD      HL,MDEAD2
        CALL    PSTR
        JP      XSCRAP

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; XCHKAB - has the user asked to stop?  Carry set if so.
;
; Checked once per block rather than per byte: a block is milliseconds of
; work, and this way the cost is nothing while the transfer still answers
; a keypress.  Without it, a transfer whose peer has walked away can only
; be waited out through every retry - which is the one moment a person is
; most likely to want out.
;
; ESC or the menu key stop it; any other key is swallowed, because a
; stray keystroke during a transfer should not end it.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
XCHKAB: CALL    CST
        RET     Z               ; nothing typed: carry clear
        CALL    CIN
        CP      03H             ; ^C - what a user reaches for to back out
        JR      Z,XCHKA1
        CP      ESC
        JR      Z,XCHKA1
        LD      HL,ESCCH
        CP      (HL)
        JR      Z,XCHKA1
        XOR     A               ; some other key: ignore it
        RET
XCHKA1: SCF
        RET

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; XUSER - the user stopped it.  Tell the far end with the protocol's own
; cancel, then clean up exactly as a failure does.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
XUSER:  CALL    XCANC
        CALL    RSTDMA
        CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MXUSER
        CALL    PSTR
        JP      XSCRAP

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; XFNAME - prompt for a filename and build an FCB from it.
;
; Accepts an optional drive letter (B:PROG.COM), folds to upper case, and
; pads the name and type with spaces the way CP/M expects.  Carry set if
; the user typed nothing, so RETURN always means "never mind".
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
XFNAME: LD      A,20            ; room for drive + 8.3 + slack
        LD      (XLINE),A
        LD      DE,XLINE
        LD      C,BRDBUF
        CALL    BDOS
        CALL    CRLF
        LD      A,(XLINE+1)     ; how many characters were typed
        OR      A
        JP      Z,XFNBAD        ; bare RETURN: never mind
        LD      B,A
        LD      HL,XLINE+2
        ; ^C anywhere in the line cancels as well.  A user who has thought
        ; better of a transfer reaches for ^C, not RETURN, and without this the
        ; 03H is taken as part of a filename - opening some other file, or
        ; creating one with a control character in its name.
XFNCC:  LD      A,(HL)
        CP      03H
        JP      Z,XFNBAD
        INC     HL
        DEC     B
        JR      NZ,XFNCC
        LD      A,(XLINE+1)     ; rewind for the parse proper
        LD      B,A
        LD      HL,XLINE+2
        XOR     A               ; clear the FCB: drive 0, spaces, no extent
        LD      (XFCB),A
        LD      DE,XFCB+1
        LD      A,' '
        LD      C,11
XFN1:   LD      (DE),A
        INC     DE
        DEC     C
        JR      NZ,XFN1
        XOR     A
        LD      (XFCB+12),A
        LD      (XFCB+13),A
        LD      (XFCB+14),A
        LD      (XFCB+15),A
        LD      (XFCB+32),A     ; current record
        ; a drive letter?
        LD      A,B
        CP      2
        JR      C,XFN2
        LD      A,(XLINE+3)
        CP      ':'
        JR      NZ,XFN2
        LD      A,(XLINE+2)
        CALL    UPCASE
        SUB     'A'-1           ; FCB drive is 1-based (1 = A:)
        LD      (XFCB),A
        INC     HL
        INC     HL
        DEC     B
        DEC     B
        ; name, up to 8 characters, then the type after a dot
XFN2:   LD      DE,XFCB+1
        LD      C,8
XFN3:   LD      A,B
        OR      A
        JR      Z,XFNOK
        LD      A,(HL)
        CP      '.'
        JR      Z,XFN4
        CALL    XWILD           ; one file, named exactly
        JP      C,XFNBAD
        CALL    UPCASE
        LD      (DE),A
        INC     DE
        INC     HL
        DEC     B
        DEC     C
        JR      NZ,XFN3
XFN3A:  LD      A,B             ; name full: skip to the dot
        OR      A
        JR      Z,XFNOK
        LD      A,(HL)
        INC     HL
        DEC     B
        CP      '.'
        JR      NZ,XFN3A
XFN4:   LD      A,(HL)          ; step over the dot itself
        CP      '.'
        JR      NZ,XFN5
        INC     HL
        DEC     B
XFN5:   LD      DE,XFCB+9
        LD      C,3
XFN6:   LD      A,B
        OR      A
        JR      Z,XFNOK
        LD      A,(HL)
        CALL    XWILD
        JP      C,XFNBAD
        CALL    UPCASE
        LD      (DE),A
        INC     DE
        INC     HL
        DEC     B
        DEC     C
        JR      NZ,XFN6
XFNOK:  LD      A,(XFCB+1)      ; a name of nothing but spaces is no name
        CP      ' '
        JR      Z,XFNBAD
        XOR     A               ; carry clear: the FCB is ready
        RET
XFNBAD: CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MXNONAM
        CALL    PSTR
        SCF
        RET

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; XWILD - carry set if the character in A is a wildcard.
;
; XMODEM moves one file with no name of its own, so '*' and '?' have
; nothing to match against: passed into an FCB they would open some
; arbitrary file to send, or create a file literally called '?' on a
; receive.  Refusing them is kinder than either.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
XWILD:  CP      '*'
        JR      Z,XWILD1
        CP      '?'
        JR      Z,XWILD1
        RET                     ; carry clear from CP with no match
XWILD1: SCF
        RET

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; XDIRTS / XDIRTC - mark and clear "changed but not saved".
;
; Every setting takes effect the moment it is chosen, which is what makes
; the menus feel right - but nothing is written to disk until V.  Without
; a marker that is easy to misread as "my settings are not being saved",
; so the menu says so plainly while a change is pending.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
XDIRTS: LD      A,1
        LD      (XDIRTY),A
        RET
XDIRTC: XOR     A
        LD      (XDIRTY),A
        RET

;=======================================================================
; SETTINGS PERSISTENCE
;
; The settings live in a block inside this .COM, and saving them means
; writing that part of the file back over itself.  No second file to
; carry around, and a copy of EGT80.COM taken to another machine brings
; its settings with it - the way period software did it.
;
; Only the ONE 128-byte record holding the block is rewritten, using
; random-record write.  The record's contents are simply the memory it was
; loaded from, so the DMA address is pointed straight at the aligned image
; address and nothing has to be copied.
;
; CP/M never tells a program its own name, so the filename below is fixed:
; if EGT80.COM has been renamed, or is not on the current drive, the save
; is refused with a message rather than writing to something else.
;=======================================================================
SAVCFG: LD      DE,CFGFCB
        LD      C,BOPEN
        CALL    BDOS
        INC     A               ; 0FFH = not found
        JR      Z,SAVERR
        LD      A,CFGREC        ; which record the block sits in
        LD      (CFGFCB+33),A   ; random-record number, low byte
        XOR     A
        LD      (CFGFCB+34),A
        LD      (CFGFCB+35),A
        LD      DE,CFGADR       ; the aligned image address = the record
        LD      C,BSETDMA
        CALL    BDOS
        LD      DE,CFGFCB
        LD      C,BWRRAN
        CALL    BDOS
        OR      A
        JR      NZ,SAVERR
        LD      DE,CFGFCB
        LD      C,BCLOSE
        CALL    BDOS
        CALL    RSTDMA
        CALL    XDIRTC          ; what is on disk now matches what is in RAM
        CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MSAVED
        CALL    PSTR
        RET
SAVERR: CALL    RSTDMA
        CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MSAVERR
        CALL    PSTR
        RET

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; RSTDMA - put the DMA address back where CP/M left it (0080H).  A
; program that moves it and does not restore it leaves the next BDOS file
; call writing into whatever it pointed at.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
RSTDMA: LD      DE,0080H
        LD      C,BSETDMA
        JP      BDOS

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; CFGCHK - validate the settings block at startup.
;
; The signature guards against a patch written by something else, and the
; values are range-checked because a plausible-looking block with a
; nonsense port kind would send PORTST somewhere it should not go.  Any
; failure falls back to the defaults, so a damaged or hand-edited copy of
; EGT80.COM still starts in a usable state instead of misbehaving.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
CFGCHK: LD      HL,CFGSIG
        LD      DE,CFGREF
        LD      B,8
CFGCH1: LD      A,(DE)
        CP      (HL)
        JR      NZ,CFGBAD
        INC     HL
        INC     DE
        DEC     B
        JR      NZ,CFGCH1
        LD      A,(PKIND)       ; a kind we actually have a driver for
        CP      PKAUX+1
        JR      NC,CFGBAD
        LD      A,(FLTMOD)      ; the flags are booleans
        CP      2
        JR      NC,CFGBAD
        LD      A,(ECHOFL)
        CP      2
        JR      NC,CFGBAD
        LD      A,(CRLFFL)
        CP      2
        JR      NC,CFGBAD
        LD      A,(PABASE)      ; an ASCI base whose registers would run off
        CP      0F0H            ; the end of the I/O space is not usable
        JR      NC,CFGBAD
        LD      A,(CLSMOD)      ; one of the three clear-screen dialects
        CP      3
        JR      NC,CFGBAD
        LD      A,(LBAUD)       ; the line settings, each within its table
        CP      RATEN
        JR      NC,CFGBAD
        LD      A,(LBITS)
        CP      7
        JR      C,CFGBAD
        CP      9
        JR      NC,CFGBAD
        LD      A,(LPAR)
        CP      3
        JR      NC,CFGBAD
        LD      A,(LSTOP)
        OR      A
        JR      Z,CFGBAD
        CP      3
        JR      NC,CFGBAD
        LD      A,(LADIV)
        CP      3
        JR      NC,CFGBAD
        LD      A,(LSAPP)
        CP      2
        JR      NC,CFGBAD
        LD      A,(ESCCH)       ; a menu key that is not a usable control key
        CALL    ESCOK           ; would trap that key for ever - and a letter
        JR      C,CFGBAD        ; would swallow ordinary typing
        RET
CFGBAD: LD      A,PKNONE        ; safe defaults: nothing selected, ANSI
        LD      (PKIND),A
        XOR     A
        LD      (PABASE),A
        LD      (PCHAN),A
        LD      (FLTMOD),A
        LD      (ECHOFL),A
        LD      (CRLFFL),A
        LD      (CLSMOD),A      ; ^Z clear, the portable default
        LD      (LPAR),A        ; 9600 8N1, and programming switched off -
        LD      (LSAPP),A       ; the values are only a starting point while
        LD      A,5             ; LSAPP is 0, but they must still be legal
        LD      (LBAUD),A       ; because the screen displays them
        LD      A,8
        LD      (LBITS),A
        LD      A,1
        LD      (LSTOP),A
        LD      (LADIV),A
        LD      A,19H           ; ^Y
        LD      (ESCCH),A
        CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MCFGBAD
        CALL    PSTR
        JP      TWAIT           ; the menu clears the screen: make sure this
                                ; is read before it goes

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; ESCOK - is A a usable menu key?  Carry clear if it is.
;
; It has to be a control key: a letter would be swallowed the moment the
; user typed it in terminal mode.  Four control keys are refused for
; reasons of their own - ^C backs out of every screen here, CR and LF are
; ordinary typing, TAB is wanted by full-screen software, and ESC begins
; the arrow-key sequences, so it would fire on a cursor key.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
ESCOK:  OR      A
        JR      Z,ESCNO         ; NUL is not a key anyone can press
        CP      20H
        JR      NC,ESCNO        ; not a control key at all
        CP      03H
        JR      Z,ESCNO
        CP      TAB
        JR      Z,ESCNO
        CP      LF
        JR      Z,ESCNO
        CP      CR
        JR      Z,ESCNO
        CP      ESC
        JR      Z,ESCNO
        OR      A               ; A is known non-zero, so this only clears
        RET                     ; carry - which a CP does NOT do: every CP
                                ; above a control code leaves it set
ESCNO:  SCF
        RET

;=======================================================================
; SCREENS
;=======================================================================

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; CLS - clear the screen, in whichever dialect this console understands.
;
; CP/M has no standard for this: the terminal is not the computer's, and
; the BIOS offers nothing.  Period software therefore either asked or was
; built per terminal.  EGT80 asks, and defaults to the ADM-3A's ^Z, which
; is the widest answer - most CP/M-era terminals took it, and the gateway's
; own terminal translation recognises it and re-renders the clear for
; whatever is really connected, PETSCII C64 included.  A VT100 or a modern
; emulator wants the ANSI form instead, and `off` is there for a printing
; terminal, where clearing the screen means feeding paper.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
CLS:    LD      A,(CLSMOD)
        OR      A
        JR      NZ,CLSANS
        LD      A,1AH           ; ADM-3A: clear screen and home the cursor
        JP      COUT
CLSANS: DEC     A
        RET     NZ              ; 2 = off: leave the screen alone
        LD      HL,MANSCL
        JP      PSTR

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; SCTTL - the title, on a freshly cleared screen.
;
; Plain text on purpose beyond the clear itself: the user may not have
; told us yet whether this console understands ANSI, and a title screen
; that sprays escape sequences at a dumb terminal is a poor first
; impression.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
SCTTL:  CALL    CLS
        CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MTITLE
        CALL    PSTR
        LD      HL,MRULE
        CALL    PSTR
        LD      HL,MVER
        CALL    PSTR
        RET

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; SCMENU - the main menu, with the current settings above it.
;
; The port and the filter mode are shown every time because they are the
; two settings that decide whether the program appears to work at all.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
SCMENU: CALL    SCTTL           ; cleared screen + banner, every time round
        CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MPORT
        CALL    PSTR
        CALL    SHPORT
        LD      HL,MMODE
        CALL    PSTR
        CALL    SHMODE
        LD      HL,MMKEY        ; say how to get back before they go in
        CALL    PSTR
        CALL    SHESC
        LD      HL,MMKEY2
        CALL    PSTR
        CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MMENU
        CALL    PSTR
        LD      A,(XDIRTY)      ; say so while a change is only in memory
        OR      A
        RET     Z
        LD      HL,MUNSAVE
        CALL    PSTR
        RET

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; SHPORT - describe the selected port in the words the board's own
; documentation uses, followed by the actual addresses.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
SHPORT: LD      A,(PKIND)
        CP      PKSIO
        JR      Z,SHPSIO
        CP      PKACIA
        JR      Z,SHPACI
        CP      PKHBIO
        JR      Z,SHPHB
        CP      PKASCI
        JR      Z,SHPASC
        CP      PKAUX
        JR      Z,SHPAUX
        LD      HL,MPNONE
        CALL    PSTR
        JP      CRLF
SHPSIO: LD      HL,MPSIO
        CALL    PSTR
        JP      SHPADR
SHPACI: LD      HL,MPACIA
        CALL    PSTR
        JP      SHPADR
SHPHB:  LD      HL,MPHBIO
        CALL    PSTR
        LD      A,(PBASE)
        ADD     A,'0'           ; the unit number, not an address
        CALL    COUT
        JP      CRLF
SHPASC: LD      HL,MPASCI
        CALL    PSTR
        LD      A,(PCHAN)
        ADD     A,'0'
        CALL    COUT
        LD      HL,MPBASE
        CALL    PSTR
        LD      A,(PABASE)
        CALL    PHEX
        JP      CRLF
SHPAUX: LD      HL,MPAUX
        CALL    PSTR
        JP      CRLF

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; SHPADR - the shared "nn/nn" tail for the families that live at a pair
; of I/O addresses, printed in the form the board's documentation uses.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
SHPADR: LD      A,(PBASE)
        CALL    PHEX
        LD      A,'/'
        CALL    COUT
        LD      A,(PBASE)
        INC     A
        CALL    PHEX
        JP      CRLF

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; SHMODE - the filter mode, plus the two toggles that surprise people
; when they are set the other way.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
SHMODE: LD      A,(FLTMOD)
        OR      A
        LD      HL,MMANSI
        JR      Z,SHMOD1
        LD      HL,MMASC
SHMOD1: CALL    PSTR
        LD      A,(ECHOFL)
        OR      A
        JR      Z,SHMOD2
        LD      HL,MMECHO
        CALL    PSTR
SHMOD2: LD      A,(CRLFFL)
        OR      A
        JR      Z,SHMOD3
        LD      HL,MMCRLF
        CALL    PSTR
SHMOD3: JP      CRLF

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; SHESC - name the escape key as a caret pair, e.g. ^Y.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
SHESC:  LD      A,'^'
        CALL    COUT
        LD      A,(ESCCH)
        ADD     A,40H           ; control code to its printable letter
        JP      COUT


;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; SCPORT - port selection, in two steps: the family, then which one.
;
; Families are listed with the machines they belong to rather than by
; chip name alone, because the user knows what they are sitting at.  The
; wrong choice produces silence rather than an error, so the help says so
; and the menu names real machines.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
SCPORT: CALL    CLS
        CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MPNOW        ; what is in force right now
        CALL    PSTR
        CALL    SHPORT
        CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MPORTS
        CALL    PSTR
SCPOR1: CALL    CIN
        CALL    UPCASE
        CP      'D'
        JP      Z,SCPDEF
        CP      '1'
        JP      Z,SCPSIO
        CP      '2'
        JP      Z,SCPACI
        CP      '3'
        JP      Z,SCPHB
        CP      '4'
        JP      Z,SCPASC
        CP      '5'
        JP      Z,SCPAUX
        CP      'N'
        JR      Z,SCPONO
        CP      'Q'
        RET     Z
        CP      03H             ; ^C backs out too
        RET     Z
        LD      A,BEL
        CALL    COUT
        JR      SCPOR1
SCPONO: XOR     A
        LD      (PKIND),A
        JP      PDONE

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; SCPDEF - the default port: Z80 SIO/2 board 1 channel B, 82/83.
;
; This is the pairing that works with the Ethernet Gateway out of the box
; (its own default virtual-modem port is the same one), so it is the answer
; to "I changed something and now nothing connects".  It resets the whole
; port group - kind, address, ASCI channel and I/O base - so no leftover
; from another family can be left pointing somewhere odd.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
SCPDEF: LD      A,PKSIO
        LD      (PKIND),A
        LD      A,82H
        LD      (PBASE),A
        XOR     A
        LD      (PCHAN),A
        LD      (PABASE),A
        CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MPDEF
        CALL    PSTR
        JP      PDONE

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; SCPSIO - Z80 SIO/2: four channels across two boards, or any address.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
SCPSIO: CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MPSIOM
        CALL    PSTR
SCPSI1: CALL    CIN
        CALL    UPCASE
        CP      '1'
        JR      Z,SCPS80
        CP      '2'
        JR      Z,SCPS82
        CP      '3'
        JR      Z,SCPS84
        CP      '4'
        JR      Z,SCPS86
        CP      'B'
        JR      Z,SCPSBA
        CP      'Q'
        RET     Z
        CP      03H             ; ^C backs out too
        RET     Z
        LD      A,BEL
        CALL    COUT
        JR      SCPSI1
SCPS80: LD      A,80H
        JR      SCPSSE
SCPS82: LD      A,82H
        JR      SCPSSE
SCPS84: LD      A,84H
        JR      SCPSSE
SCPS86: LD      A,86H
        JR      SCPSSE
SCPSBA: CALL    HEXASK          ; any address the board happens to use
        RET     C               ; cancelled: leave the port alone
SCPSSE: LD      (PBASE),A
        LD      A,PKSIO
        LD      (PKIND),A
        JP      PDONE

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; SCPACI - 6850 ACIA: the two Altair 88-2SIO ports, or any address.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
SCPACI: CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MPACIM
        CALL    PSTR
SCPAC1: CALL    CIN
        CALL    UPCASE
        CP      '1'
        JR      Z,SCPA10
        CP      '2'
        JR      Z,SCPA12
        CP      'B'
        JR      Z,SCPABA
        CP      'Q'
        RET     Z
        CP      03H             ; ^C backs out too
        RET     Z
        LD      A,BEL
        CALL    COUT
        JR      SCPAC1
SCPA10: LD      A,10H
        JR      SCPASE
SCPA12: LD      A,12H
        JR      SCPASE
SCPABA: CALL    HEXASK
        RET     C
SCPASE: LD      (PBASE),A
        LD      A,PKACIA
        LD      (PKIND),A
        JP      PDONE

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; SCPHB - RomWBW HBIOS: a unit number, not an address.
;
; Checked before it is accepted: HBIOS is reached by RST 8, and on a
; machine without it that instruction lands in unused memory and takes
; the program with it.  A RomWBW system puts a JP there, so the absence
; of one is a reliable "this is not a RomWBW machine" - far better to say
; so than to hang on the first keystroke.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
SCPHB:  LD      A,(0008H)       ; the RST 8 vector
        CP      0C3H            ; a JP instruction?
        JR      Z,SCPHB1
        CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MNOHB
        CALL    PSTR
        JP      TWAIT           ; Settings clears on return: hold it here
SCPHB1: CALL    CLS
        CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MHBUNM       ; heading, then one line per unit
        CALL    PSTR
        XOR     A
        LD      (HBSCAN),A
SCPHBL: LD      HL,MHBIND       ; '  n  '
        CALL    PSTR
        LD      A,(HBSCAN)
        ADD     A,'0'
        CALL    COUT
        LD      A,' '
        CALL    COUT
        LD      A,' '
        CALL    COUT
        LD      A,(HBSCAN)
        CALL    HBDEV           ; ask the firmware what this unit is
        JR      C,SCPHBN
        CALL    SHTYPE
        LD      HL,MHBBAS
        CALL    PSTR
        LD      A,(HBBASE)
        CALL    PHEX
        CALL    CRLF
        JR      SCPHBI
SCPHBN: LD      HL,MHBNON       ; refused: not present on this machine
        CALL    PSTR
SCPHBI: LD      A,(HBSCAN)
        INC     A
        LD      (HBSCAN),A
        CP      4               ; units 0-3, as before
        JR      C,SCPHBL
        LD      HL,MPHBM        ; the prompt itself
        CALL    PSTR
SCPHB2: CALL    CIN
        CALL    UPCASE
        CP      'Q'
        RET     Z
        CP      03H             ; ^C backs out too
        RET     Z
        SUB     '0'
        CP      4               ; units 0-3
        JR      C,SCPHBS
        LD      A,BEL
        CALL    COUT
        JR      SCPHB2
SCPHBS: LD      (PBASE),A
        LD      A,PKHBIO
        LD      (PKIND),A
        JP      PDONE

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; SCPASC - Z180 ASCI: a channel, and the internal I/O base if the system
; has moved it (RomWBW does on some boards).
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
SCPASC: CALL    ISZ180          ; the ASCI is part of the CPU: is it there?
        JR      NC,SCPASF
        CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MNOZ180
        CALL    PSTR
        JP      TWAIT           ; as above: read before the screen clears
SCPASF: CALL    ASCAUT          ; let the firmware fill the base in, if it can
SCPAS2: CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MPASCM
        CALL    PSTR
        LD      HL,MASCBAS      ; the base in force, so it is never a mystery
        CALL    PSTR
        LD      A,(PABASE)
        CALL    PHEX
        CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MASCPR
        CALL    PSTR
SCPAS1: CALL    CIN
        CALL    UPCASE
        CP      '0'
        JR      Z,SCPAS0
        CP      '1'
        JR      Z,SCPASA
        CP      'C'
        JP      Z,SCASC0
        CP      'R'
        JP      Z,SCASCR
        CP      'B'
        JP      Z,SCPASB
        CP      'Q'
        RET     Z
        CP      03H             ; ^C backs out too
        RET     Z
        LD      A,BEL
        CALL    COUT
        JR      SCPAS1
SCPAS0: XOR     A
        JP      SCPASS
SCPASA: LD      A,1
        JP      SCPASS
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; SCASC0 - the base RomWBW uses on Small Computer Central boards.
;
; The Z180's serial registers are inside the CPU, and the whole internal
; register block is relocatable - the ICR decides where it lands.  RomWBW
; moves it to C0 on these boards, so a driver that pokes the registers
; directly has to be told; the 00 this program defaults to addresses
; nothing there, and the symptom is the family's usual silence.  It is
; offered as a named choice because "C0" is knowledge about someone else's
; firmware, not something a user should have to look up to type into a hex
; prompt.  (Reaching the port through HBIOS instead avoids the question
; entirely: the firmware knows where its own registers are.)
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
SCASC0: LD      A,0C0H
        LD      (PABASE),A
        CALL    XDIRTS
        CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MASCC0
        CALL    PSTR
        LD      HL,MASCPIK      ; setting a base selects no port on its own
        CALL    PSTR
        JP      SCPAS2

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; ASCAUT - fill in the I/O base from RomWBW on the way into this menu.
;
; The base was a step the user had to know existed: pick the family, then
; realise the registers are somewhere else, then find the right number.  On
; a RomWBW machine none of that is necessary - the firmware will say - so it
; is asked here and the answer used, silently, before the menu is drawn.
; Nothing is reported because there is nothing to decide; the menu shows the
; base in force, and R re-asks with an explanation if anyone wants to see it.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
ASCAUT: CALL    ASCFND          ; carry set: no HBIOS, or no ASCI unit listed
        RET     C
        LD      HL,PABASE
        CP      (HL)
        RET     Z               ; already right: leave the dirty flag alone
        LD      (HL),A
        JP      XDIRTS

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; ASCFND - scan the HBIOS character units for an ASCI one and return the
; base of its register block in A.  Carry set if there is nothing to ask or
; no ASCI unit among them.
;
; The base HBIOS reports belongs to a physical channel, and channel 1 sits
; one address above channel 0 (RomWBW: ASCI1_BASE = Z180_BASE + 1), so the
; channel it also reports is subtracted to recover the block base - the
; number this program adds the channel back to when it addresses a
; register.  Off by one there would put every register one byte out.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
ASCFND: XOR     A
        LD      (HBSCAN),A
ASCFN1: LD      A,(HBSCAN)
        CALL    HBDEV
        JR      C,ASCFN2
        LD      A,(HBTYPE)
        CP      01H             ; the published ASCI device type
        JR      Z,ASCFN3
ASCFN2: LD      A,(HBSCAN)
        INC     A
        LD      (HBSCAN),A
        CP      4
        JR      C,ASCFN1
        SCF
        RET
ASCFN3: LD      A,(HBBASE)
        LD      E,A
        LD      A,(HBPHYS)
        CP      E               ; a channel above its own base is nonsense
        JR      Z,ASCFN4
        JR      NC,ASCFN2
ASCFN4: LD      A,E
        LD      HL,HBPHYS
        SUB     (HL)
        CP      0F0H            ; base+9 must stay inside the I/O space
        JR      NC,ASCFN2
        OR      A               ; carry clear: A holds the block base
        RET

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; SCASCR - ask RomWBW where the ASCI registers are, rather than assume.
;
; C0 is right for most RomWBW Z180 platforms but not all - the N8, MK4 and
; RPH configurations put the block at 40 - so guessing a constant is only
; ever nearly right.  The firmware knows: CIODEVICE reports each unit's
; device type and base address, so scan the units for an ASCI one and take
; the base it names.
;
; The reported base belongs to that *physical channel*, and channel 1 sits
; one address above channel 0 (RomWBW: ASCI1_BASE = Z180_BASE + 1), so the
; channel number it also reports is subtracted to recover the block base -
; which is what this program adds the channel to when it addresses a
; register.  Getting that wrong would put every register one byte out.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
SCASCR: CALL    ASCFND
        JR      C,SCASRN
        LD      (PABASE),A
        CALL    XDIRTS
        CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MASCGOT
        CALL    PSTR
        LD      A,(PABASE)
        CALL    PHEX
        CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MASCPIK
        CALL    PSTR
        JP      SCPAS2
SCASRN: CALL    CRLF            ; nothing to ask, or no ASCI unit listed
        LD      HL,MASCNOR
        CALL    PSTR
        CALL    TWAIT
        JP      SCPAS2
SCPASB: CALL    HEXASK          ; internal I/O base
        RET     C
        CP      0F0H            ; base+9 must not run off the top of the
        JR      NC,SCPASB       ; I/O space: ask again
        LD      (PABASE),A
        JP      SCPAS2          ; then still ask for the channel
SCPASS: LD      (PCHAN),A
        LD      A,PKASCI
        LD      (PKIND),A
        JP      PDONE

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; SCPAUX - the CP/M AUX device: nothing to configure.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
SCPAUX: LD      A,PKAUX
        LD      (PKIND),A
        CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MAUXW
        CALL    PSTR
        CALL    TWAIT           ; a caveat worth reading, not flashing past
        JP      PDONE

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; PDONE - re-point the vectors at the new selection and show it.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
PDONE:  CALL    XDIRTS          ; the port changed but is not on disk yet
        CALL    PORTST
        CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MPSET
        CALL    PSTR
        JP      SHPORT

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; HEXASK - read a two-digit hex byte.  Returns it in A, or carry set if
; the user gave up (RETURN or a bad digit), so a mistake never leaves a
; half-entered address behind.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
HEXASK: LD      HL,MHEXA
        CALL    PSTR
        CALL    HEXDIG
        RET     C
        ADD     A,A
        ADD     A,A
        ADD     A,A
        ADD     A,A             ; high nibble
        LD      (HEXACC),A
        CALL    HEXDIG
        RET     C
        LD      HL,HEXACC
        OR      (HL)
        RET                     ; carry clear: A holds the byte

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; HEXDIG - read one hex digit, echo it, return its value in A.  Carry set
; on anything that is not a hex digit.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
HEXDIG: CALL    CIN
        CALL    UPCASE
        CP      '0'
        JR      C,HEXBAD
        CP      '9'+1
        JR      C,HEXNUM
        CP      'A'
        JR      C,HEXBAD
        CP      'F'+1
        JR      NC,HEXBAD
        CALL    COUT
        SUB     'A'-10
        RET                     ; carry clear from SUB of a smaller value
HEXNUM: CALL    COUT
        SUB     '0'
        RET
HEXBAD: SCF
        RET

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; SCSETT - settings.  Each key toggles and the screen redraws, so the
; effect of a keypress is always visible.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
SCSETT: CALL    CLS
        CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MSETT
        CALL    PSTR
        CALL    SHMODE
        LD      HL,MSPORT       ; the port lives here now, so show it here
        CALL    PSTR
        CALL    SHPORT
        LD      HL,MSKEY
        CALL    PSTR
        CALL    SHESC
        CALL    CRLF            ; SHESC names the key and stops there
        LD      HL,MSCLS
        CALL    PSTR
        CALL    SHCLS
        LD      HL,MSETT2
        CALL    PSTR
SCSET1: CALL    CIN
        CALL    UPCASE
        CP      'P'
        JR      Z,DOSPRT
        CP      'A'
        JR      Z,TOGANS
        CP      'E'
        JR      Z,TOGECH
        CP      'L'
        JR      Z,TOGCRL
        CP      'K'
        JR      Z,CHGESC
        CP      'B'
        JR      Z,DOSLIN
        CP      'C'
        JR      Z,CYCCLS
        CP      'Q'
        RET     Z
        CP      03H             ; ^C backs out too
        RET     Z
        LD      A,BEL
        CALL    COUT
        JR      SCSET1
DOSPRT: CALL    SCPORT
        JR      SCSETT          ; back to Settings, showing the new port
DOSLIN: CALL    SCLINE
        JR      SCSETT
TOGANS: LD      HL,FLTMOD
        CALL    TOGGLE
        JP      SCSETT
TOGECH: LD      HL,ECHOFL
        CALL    TOGGLE
        JP      SCSETT
TOGCRL: LD      HL,CRLFFL
        CALL    TOGGLE
        JP      SCSETT
TOGGLE: LD      A,(HL)
        XOR     1
        LD      (HL),A
        JP      XDIRTS          ; a toggle is a change too

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; CHGESC - set the menu key to whichever control key the user presses.
;
; A cycle through three fixed choices was not enough: which key is free
; depends on the remote system, not on us (^Y is WordStar's delete-line,
; ^] is telnet's escape, ^\ is Kermit's, and something wants each of
; them).  So the user presses the key they want and we take it, subject to
; ESCOK, which refuses the handful that cannot work.  An unusable key is
; explained rather than beeped at: "nothing happened" is indistinguishable
; from a broken keyboard.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
CHGESC: CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MESCAS
        CALL    PSTR
        CALL    CIN
        CP      03H             ; ^C: changed my mind
        JP      Z,SCSETT
        CALL    ESCOK
        JR      C,ESCREF
        LD      (ESCCH),A
        CALL    XDIRTS
        JP      SCSETT          ; the redraw names the new key
ESCREF: CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MESCNO
        CALL    PSTR
        CALL    TWAIT
        JP      SCSETT

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; CYCCLS - step the clear-screen dialect: ADM-3A ^Z, ANSI, off.
;
; A cycle rather than a submenu: there are only three answers, and the
; effect shows on the very next redraw - which is the quickest way to find
; the one a given terminal understands.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
CYCCLS: LD      A,(CLSMOD)
        INC     A
        CP      3
        JR      C,CYCCL1
        XOR     A
CYCCL1: LD      (CLSMOD),A
        CALL    XDIRTS
        JP      SCSETT

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; SHCLS - name the clear-screen dialect in force.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
SHCLS:  LD      A,(CLSMOD)
        OR      A
        LD      HL,MCLSZ
        JR      Z,SHCLS1
        DEC     A
        LD      HL,MCLSAN
        JR      Z,SHCLS1
        LD      HL,MCLSOF
SHCLS1: CALL    PSTR
        JP      CRLF

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; SCLINE - line settings: speed and framing.
;
; What a terminal can actually set depends entirely on the family, and
; this screen says so rather than offering knobs that do nothing:
;
;   RomWBW HBIOS  a firmware call sets speed AND framing (CIOINIT).
;   Z180 ASCI     the rate really is in the CPU's own registers.
;   6850 ACIA     framing is in the control register; the rate is only
;                 the board clock divided by 1, 16 or 64.
;   Z80 SIO/2     nothing: the SIO has no baud generator at all.  The bit
;                 rate comes from the board's clock or CTC, and the chip's
;                 registers are write-only, so we cannot even read what
;                 the ROM chose - reprogramming it would be a guess with a
;                 dead port as the prize.
;   CP/M AUX:     nothing: the operating system owns the port.
;
; And against the Ethernet Gateway's own virtual modem none of it applies
; either way - a TCP connection has no bit rate.  Hence R: EGT80 programs
; nothing at all unless asked, which is both the shipped state and the way
; back from a setting that made a real port stop answering.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
SCLINE: CALL    CLS
        CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MLINE
        CALL    PSTR
        LD      HL,MSPORT
        CALL    PSTR
        CALL    SHPORT
        LD      HL,MLRATE
        CALL    PSTR
        CALL    SHRATE
        LD      HL,MLFMT
        CALL    PSTR
        CALL    SHFMT
        CALL    SHAPP
        LD      HL,MLINE2
        CALL    PSTR
SCLIN1: CALL    CIN
        CALL    UPCASE
        CP      'B'
        JP      Z,SCBAUD
        CP      'D'
        JP      Z,SCFMT
        CP      'A'
        JR      Z,SCLAPP
        CP      'R'
        JR      Z,SCLDEF
        CP      'Q'
        RET     Z
        CP      03H             ; ^C backs out too
        RET     Z
        LD      A,BEL
        CALL    COUT
        JR      SCLIN1
SCLAPP: CALL    LSAPLY          ; apply, and say what happened
        CALL    TWAIT
        JP      SCLINE
SCLDEF: XOR     A
        LD      (LSAPP),A       ; stop programming the port
        LD      (LPAR),A
        LD      A,5
        LD      (LBAUD),A       ; 9600 8N1 as the displayed starting point
        LD      A,8
        LD      (LBITS),A
        LD      A,1
        LD      (LSTOP),A
        LD      (LADIV),A
        CALL    XDIRTS
        CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MLDEF
        CALL    PSTR
        CALL    TWAIT
        JP      SCLINE

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; SHRATE - the speed, in the only terms that are true for this family.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
SHRATE: LD      A,(PKIND)
        CP      PKSIO
        JR      Z,SHRSIO
        CP      PKAUX
        JR      Z,SHRAUX
        CP      PKACIA
        JR      Z,SHRACI
        CP      PKNONE
        JR      Z,SHRNON
        CALL    PRATE           ; HBIOS and ASCI: a real number of bits/s
        LD      A,(PKIND)
        CP      PKASCI
        JR      NZ,SHRAT1
        LD      HL,MLASCLK      ; ...on the clock the table assumes
        CALL    PSTR
SHRAT1: JP      CRLF
SHRSIO: LD      HL,MLRSIO
        CALL    PSTR
        JP      CRLF
SHRAUX: LD      HL,MLRAUX
        CALL    PSTR
        JP      CRLF
SHRNON: LD      HL,MLRNON
        CALL    PSTR
        JP      CRLF
SHRACI: LD      HL,MLRACI       ; a divider, not a rate: say divider
        CALL    PSTR
        LD      A,(LADIV)
        OR      A
        LD      HL,MLD1
        JR      Z,SHRAC1
        DEC     A
        LD      HL,MLD16
        JR      Z,SHRAC1
        LD      HL,MLD64
SHRAC1: CALL    PSTR
        JP      CRLF

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; PRATE - print the selected rate from the table.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
PRATE:  LD      A,(LBAUD)
        ADD     A,A             ; two bytes per pointer
        LD      E,A
        LD      D,0
        LD      HL,RATEPT
        ADD     HL,DE
        LD      E,(HL)
        INC     HL
        LD      D,(HL)
        EX      DE,HL
        JP      PSTR

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; SHFMT - the framing, in words rather than a code.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
SHFMT:  LD      A,(LBITS)
        ADD     A,'0'
        CALL    COUT
        LD      HL,MFDATA
        CALL    PSTR
        LD      A,(LPAR)
        OR      A
        LD      HL,MFPNO
        JR      Z,SHFMT1
        DEC     A
        LD      HL,MFPEV
        JR      Z,SHFMT1
        LD      HL,MFPOD
SHFMT1: CALL    PSTR
        LD      A,(LSTOP)
        ADD     A,'0'
        CALL    COUT
        LD      HL,MFSTOP
        CALL    PSTR
        JP      CRLF

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; SHAPP - whether any of it is actually being written to the port.  This
; is the line that answers "I set 9600 and nothing changed".
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
SHAPP:  LD      A,(LSAPP)
        OR      A
        LD      HL,MLAPPN
        JR      Z,SHAPP1
        LD      HL,MLAPPY
SHAPP1: CALL    PSTR
        JP      CRLF

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; SCBAUD - pick the speed.  The list is the family's list: real rates
; where the family has a rate to set, the three 6850 dividers where that
; is all the chip has, and an explanation where there is nothing.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
SCBAUD: LD      A,(PKIND)
        CP      PKACIA
        JR      Z,SCBACI
        CP      PKHBIO
        JR      Z,SCBRAT
        CP      PKASCI
        JR      Z,SCBRAT
        CALL    CRLF            ; SIO, AUX or nothing selected
        LD      HL,MLNOSET
        CALL    PSTR
        CALL    TWAIT
        JP      SCLINE
SCBRAT: CALL    CLS
        CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MBAUDM
        CALL    PSTR
SCBRA1: CALL    CIN
        CALL    UPCASE
        CP      'Q'
        JP      Z,SCLINE
        CP      03H
        JP      Z,SCLINE
        SUB     'A'
        CP      RATEN           ; A..J, one per table entry
        JR      NC,SCBRA2
        LD      (LBAUD),A
        CALL    XDIRTS
        JP      SCLINE
SCBRA2: LD      A,BEL
        CALL    COUT
        JR      SCBRA1
SCBACI: CALL    CLS
        CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MACIDM
        CALL    PSTR
SCBAC1: CALL    CIN
        CALL    UPCASE
        CP      'Q'
        JP      Z,SCLINE
        CP      03H
        JP      Z,SCLINE
        CP      '1'
        JR      Z,SCBAC2
        CP      '2'
        JR      Z,SCBAC3
        CP      '3'
        JR      Z,SCBAC4
        LD      A,BEL
        CALL    COUT
        JR      SCBAC1
SCBAC2: XOR     A               ; /1
        JR      SCBAC5
SCBAC3: LD      A,1             ; /16
        JR      SCBAC5
SCBAC4: LD      A,2             ; /64
SCBAC5: LD      (LADIV),A
        CALL    XDIRTS
        JP      SCLINE

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; SCFMT - data bits, parity and stop bits.  One key each, and the screen
; redraws, so the current framing is always the thing on the screen.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
SCFMT:  CALL    CLS
        CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MFMTM
        CALL    PSTR
        CALL    SHFMT
        LD      HL,MFMTM2
        CALL    PSTR
SCFMT2: CALL    CIN
        CALL    UPCASE
        CP      '7'
        JR      Z,SCFMT7
        CP      '8'
        JR      Z,SCFMT8
        CP      'N'
        JR      Z,SCFMTN
        CP      'E'
        JR      Z,SCFMTE
        CP      'O'
        JR      Z,SCFMTO
        CP      '1'
        JR      Z,SCFMS1
        CP      '2'
        JR      Z,SCFMS2
        CP      'Q'
        JP      Z,SCLINE
        CP      03H
        JP      Z,SCLINE
        LD      A,BEL
        CALL    COUT
        JR      SCFMT2
SCFMT7: LD      A,7
        LD      (LBITS),A
        JR      SCFMTD
SCFMT8: LD      A,8
        LD      (LBITS),A
        JR      SCFMTD
SCFMTN: XOR     A
        LD      (LPAR),A
        JR      SCFMTD
SCFMTE: LD      A,1
        LD      (LPAR),A
        JR      SCFMTD
SCFMTO: LD      A,2
        LD      (LPAR),A
        JR      SCFMTD
SCFMS1: LD      A,1
        LD      (LSTOP),A
        JR      SCFMTD
SCFMS2: LD      A,2
        LD      (LSTOP),A
SCFMTD: CALL    XDIRTS
        JP      SCFMT

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; LSAUTO - apply the line settings if the user has asked for them to be
; applied, silently.  Called from PORTST, so it runs at startup and again
; whenever the port is re-selected - which is what makes this a setting
; rather than a one-shot command.  Silent because at startup the menu is
; about to clear the screen, and because a failure is visible on the Line
; settings screen, which reports it properly when A is pressed there.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
LSAUTO: LD      A,(LSAPP)
        OR      A
        RET     Z
        LD      A,1             ; ask LSAPP1 not to print
        LD      (LSQUIT),A
        CALL    LSAPP1
        XOR     A
        LD      (LSQUIT),A
        RET

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; LSAPLY - apply now, from the Line settings screen, and report.  A
; successful apply is also what turns programming on for good.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
LSAPLY: XOR     A
        LD      (LSQUIT),A
        CALL    LSAPP1
        RET     C               ; it could not be done: leave LSAPP alone
        LD      A,1
        LD      (LSAPP),A
        JP      XDIRTS

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; LSAPP1 - the apply itself, per family.  Carry set if nothing was done.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
LSAPP1: LD      A,(PKIND)
        CP      PKHBIO
        JR      Z,LSHB
        CP      PKACIA
        JR      Z,LSACIA
        CP      PKASCI
        JP      Z,LSASCI
        LD      HL,MLNOSET      ; SIO, AUX, or nothing selected
        CALL    LSMSG
        SCF
        RET

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; LSMSG - print a message unless we were asked to be quiet.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
LSMSG:  LD      A,(LSQUIT)
        OR      A
        RET     NZ
        PUSH    HL
        CALL    CRLF
        POP     HL
        JP      PSTR

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; LSHB - RomWBW HBIOS: one firmware call sets speed and framing together.
;
; The line-characteristics word is the published HBIOS layout: bit 13 RTS,
; bits 12-8 the baud code, bit 7 DTR, bit 4 even parity, bit 3 parity
; enable, bit 2 two stop bits, bits 1-0 the data bits as 5-8 = 0-3.  RTS
; and DTR are asserted rather than left clear, because clearing DTR on a
; real modem drops the call - the one setting here that could hang up the
; line it is being used on.
;
; The baud code is not a rate but an exponent pair: the published rule is
; V = 75 * 2^X * 3^Y with the bits laid out YXXXX, which is why the table
; jumps from 9 (38400) to 24 (57600) - 57600 is 75 * 2^8 * 3, so Y is set
; and X restarts.  RATEHB holds the codes so the arithmetic is done once,
; here, and not in the head of whoever reads this next.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
LSHB:   LD      A,(0008H)       ; a saved HBIOS setting can arrive on a
        CP      0C3H            ; machine that has no RST 8 at all - and this
        JR      NZ,LSHBNO       ; runs at startup, before the user asks for
        LD      A,(LBAUD)       ; anything, so it must check for itself
        LD      E,A
        LD      D,0
        LD      HL,RATEHB
        ADD     HL,DE
        LD      A,(HL)
        OR      20H             ; bit 13 of the word: RTS asserted
        LD      D,A
        CALL    LSFRAM          ; the framing bits, in A
        OR      80H             ; bit 7: DTR asserted
        LD      E,A
        LD      A,(PBASE)
        LD      C,A             ; the character unit
        LD      B,04H           ; CIOINIT
        RST     8
        OR      A               ; 0 = OK, anything else is a refusal
        JR      NZ,LSHBNO
        LD      HL,MLOK
        CALL    LSMSG
        OR      A               ; carry clear: done
        RET
LSHBNO: LD      HL,MLHBNO
        CALL    LSMSG
        SCF
        RET

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; LSFRAM - the HBIOS framing bits for the current format, in A.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
LSFRAM: LD      A,(LBITS)
        SUB     5               ; 5-8 data bits encode as 0-3
        LD      E,A
        LD      A,(LSTOP)
        CP      2
        JR      NZ,LSFRA1
        LD      A,E
        OR      04H             ; bit 2: two stop bits
        LD      E,A
LSFRA1: LD      A,(LPAR)
        OR      A
        JR      Z,LSFRA3        ; no parity: bits 4-3 stay clear
        LD      A,E
        OR      08H             ; bit 3: parity enabled
        LD      E,A
        LD      A,(LPAR)
        CP      1
        JR      NZ,LSFRA3       ; odd leaves bit 4 clear
        LD      A,E
        OR      10H             ; bit 4: even parity
        LD      E,A
LSFRA3: LD      A,E
        RET

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; LSACIA - 6850 ACIA: master reset, then the control register.
;
; The 6850 does not offer every combination - its word-select field has
; eight entries and 7 bits with no parity, or 8 bits with parity and two
; stop bits, are not among them.  An unavailable combination is refused
; and named rather than quietly turned into the nearest one, because a
; framing mismatch shows up as garbage characters, which is exactly the
; symptom the user came here to fix.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
LSACIA: CALL    LSAWRD          ; the word-select field, or carry set
        JR      C,LSACNO
        LD      E,A             ; hold it: bits 4-2 of the control byte
        LD      A,(LADIV)       ; the divider is bits 1-0, same order
        OR      E
        LD      E,A
        LD      A,(PBASE)
        LD      C,A
        LD      B,0
        LD      A,03H           ; master reset first, as the data sheet
        OUT     (C),A           ; requires before a control write
        LD      A,E
        OUT     (C),A
        LD      HL,MLOK
        CALL    LSMSG
        OR      A               ; carry clear
        RET
LSACNO: LD      HL,MLACNO
        CALL    LSMSG
        SCF
        RET

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; LSAWRD - the 6850 word-select bits (already shifted into 4-2) for the
; current format, or carry set if the chip has no such combination.
;
; The eight the chip does have, in its own order: 7E2 7O2 7E1 7O1 8N2
; 8N1 8E1 8O1.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
LSAWRD: LD      A,(LBITS)
        CP      8
        JR      Z,LSAW8
        LD      A,(LPAR)        ; 7 bits: parity is compulsory here
        OR      A
        JR      Z,LSAWNO
        LD      A,(LSTOP)
        CP      2
        JR      Z,LSAW72
        LD      A,(LPAR)        ; 7 bits, 1 stop
        CP      1
        LD      A,08H           ; 010 = 7E1
        JR      Z,LSAWOK
        LD      A,0CH           ; 011 = 7O1
        JR      LSAWOK
LSAW72: LD      A,(LPAR)        ; 7 bits, 2 stop
        CP      1
        LD      A,00H           ; 000 = 7E2
        JR      Z,LSAWOK
        LD      A,04H           ; 001 = 7O2
        JR      LSAWOK
LSAW8:  LD      A,(LPAR)
        OR      A
        JR      NZ,LSAW8P
        LD      A,(LSTOP)       ; 8 bits, no parity: both stop counts exist
        CP      2
        LD      A,10H           ; 100 = 8N2
        JR      Z,LSAWOK
        LD      A,14H           ; 101 = 8N1
        JR      LSAWOK
LSAW8P: LD      A,(LSTOP)       ; 8 bits with parity: only 1 stop bit
        CP      2
        JR      Z,LSAWNO
        LD      A,(LPAR)
        CP      1
        LD      A,18H           ; 110 = 8E1
        JR      Z,LSAWOK
        LD      A,1CH           ; 111 = 8O1
LSAWOK: OR      A               ; carry clear, A holds the field
        RET
LSAWNO: SCF
        RET

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; LSASCI - Z180 ASCI: the rate divider in CNTLB, the framing in CNTLA.
;
; Both are read-modify-write, because those registers hold bits that must
; not be disturbed - CNTLA carries the receiver and transmitter enables,
; and clearing either would silence the port we are configuring.  CNTLA
; bit 3 is written as 0 on purpose: that resets the latched receive error
; flags, which is harmless here and is what the error path does too.
;
; One caveat is in the silicon, not in this code: CNTLB bit 5 reads back
; as the CTS input but writes the prescaler select, so it cannot be
; preserved by a read - it is always taken from the table.
;
; The rate table assumes the 18.432 MHz crystal Z180 boards use precisely
; because it divides to the standard rates (an SC126 has one).  On a
; different clock every entry scales by the same ratio, and the screen
; says so rather than printing a number that would be wrong.
;
; REASONED, NOT RUN: our emulator's Z80 core has no IN0/OUT0, so this
; path cannot be exercised here - the same standing limitation as the rest
; of the ASCI driver.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
LSASCI: CALL    ISZ180          ; likewise: never execute IN0/OUT0 on a
        JR      C,LSASNO        ; processor that has no such instruction
        CALL    ASCPAT          ; make sure the operands match the channel
        LD      A,(LBAUD)
        LD      E,A
        LD      D,0
        LD      HL,RATEAS
        ADD     HL,DE
        LD      A,(HL)
        LD      E,A             ; E = PS, DR and SS bits for this rate
        LD      A,(LPAR)
        CP      2
        JR      NZ,LSASC1
        LD      A,E
        OR      10H             ; CNTLB bit 4: odd parity
        LD      E,A
LSASC1: DB      0EDH,38H
LSASB1: DB      00H             ; IN0 A,(CNTLBn) - operand patched
        AND     0C0H            ; keep MPBT and MP; bit 5 must not be kept
        OR      E
        DB      0EDH,39H
LSASB2: DB      00H             ; OUT0 (CNTLBn),A
        LD      A,(LBITS)       ; CNTLA: MOD2 data bits, MOD1 parity,
        CP      8               ; MOD0 stop bits
        LD      E,0
        JR      NZ,LSASC2
        LD      E,04H           ; 8 data bits
LSASC2: LD      A,(LPAR)
        OR      A
        JR      Z,LSASC3
        LD      A,E
        OR      02H             ; parity enabled
        LD      E,A
LSASC3: LD      A,(LSTOP)
        CP      2
        JR      NZ,LSASC4
        LD      A,E
        OR      01H             ; two stop bits
        LD      E,A
LSASC4: DB      0EDH,38H
LSASA1: DB      00H             ; IN0 A,(CNTLAn)
        AND     0F0H            ; keep MPE, RE, TE and RTS0; clear EFR
        OR      E
        DB      0EDH,39H
LSASA2: DB      00H             ; OUT0 (CNTLAn),A
        LD      HL,MLOK
        CALL    LSMSG
        OR      A               ; carry clear
        RET
LSASNO: LD      HL,MNOZ180
        CALL    LSMSG
        SCF
        RET

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; Rate tables.  One row per entry, and the three columns must stay in
; step: the label, the HBIOS baud code, and the Z180 CNTLB divider bits.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
RATEN   EQU     10              ; entries in each table below

; Character-device driver names, five bytes each, indexed by the device
; type HBIOS reports.  The order is the published one and must not be
; rearranged; HBTMAX is one past the last entry.
HBTNAM: DB      'UART ','ASCI ','TERM ','PRP  ','PPP  '
        DB      'SIO  ','ACIA ','PIO  ','UF   ','DUART'
        DB      'Z2U  ','LPT  ','ESPC ','ESPS ','SCON '
        DB      'SSER ','EZ80U'
HBTMAX  EQU     17

RATEPT: DW      MR300,MR600,MR1200,MR2400,MR4800
        DW      MR9600,MR19K,MR38K,MR57K,MR115K
RATEHB: DB      2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9,24,25
RATEAS: DB      2DH,2CH,2BH,2AH,29H,28H,21H,20H,01H,00H

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; SCHELP - help.  The port choice is the one thing that decides whether
; this program works, so the help says so plainly and in the same terms
; the hardware documentation uses.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
SCHELP: CALL    CLS
        LD      HL,MHELP
        CALL    PSTR
        CALL    TWAIT
        CALL    CLS             ; a page per screen: pages 2 and 3 used to
        LD      HL,MHELP2       ; scroll on below page 1, so on a 24-row
        CALL    PSTR            ; terminal the top of each was already gone
        CALL    TWAIT
        CALL    CLS
        LD      HL,MHELP3
        CALL    PSTR
        JP      TWAIT

;=======================================================================
; TEXT
;
; All user-visible text lives here.  In a program this size the strings
; are the largest single part, so keeping them together makes that cost
; visible and the wording easy to review.
;=======================================================================
MTITLE: DB      'Ethernet Gateway Terminal',CR,LF,0
MRULE:  DB      '-------------------------',CR,LF,0
MVER:   DB      'EGT80 v0.7  CP/M 2.2 / CP/M 3  Z80',CR,LF,0

MPORT:  DB      'Port: ',0
MMKEY:  DB      'Keys: ',0
MMKEY2: DB      ' leaves terminal mode for the menu; then E',CR,LF
        DB      '      returns here.',CR,LF,0
MMODE:  DB      'Mode: ',0
MPNONE: DB      'none selected - press S then P',0
MPSIO:  DB      'Z80 SIO/2 at ',0
MPACIA: DB      '6850 ACIA at ',0
MPHBIO: DB      'RomWBW HBIOS unit ',0
MPASCI: DB      'Z180 ASCI channel ',0
MPBASE: DB      ', I/O base ',0
MPAUX:  DB      'CP/M AUX: device (BDOS 3/4)',0
MMANSI: DB      'ANSI (pass escape sequences)',0
MMASC:  DB      'ASCII (strip escape sequences)',0
MMECHO: DB      ' +echo',0
MMCRLF: DB      ' +CR/LF',0

MMENU:  DB      CR,LF
        DB      '  T  Terminal mode',CR,LF
        DB      '  S  Settings  (serial port, line, display)',CR,LF
        DB      '  U  Upload a file   (XMODEM, to the far end)',CR,LF
        DB      '  D  Download a file (XMODEM, from the far end)',CR,LF
        DB      '  H  Help',CR,LF
        DB      '  V  Save settings into EGT80.COM',CR,LF
        DB      '  X  Exit to CP/M',CR,LF
        DB      CR,LF,'Choice: ',0

MPORTS: DB      'Select serial port',CR,LF
        DB      '------------------',CR,LF
        DB      '  1  Z80 SIO/2     - RC2014, RomWBW, many Z80 boards',CR,LF
        DB      '  2  6850 ACIA     - Altair 88-2SIO, RC2014 ACIA',CR,LF
        DB      '  3  RomWBW HBIOS  - any RomWBW machine (RST 8)',CR,LF
        DB      '  4  Z180 ASCI     - SC126 and other Z180 boards',CR,LF
        DB      '  5  CP/M AUX:     - portable fallback, see Help',CR,LF
        DB      '  D  Default - Z80 SIO/2 board 1 ch B, 82/83',CR,LF
        DB      '  N  None',CR,LF
        DB      '  Q  Leave unchanged  (^C also backs out)',CR,LF
        DB      CR,LF,'Choice: ',0
MPDEF:  DB      'Back to the default port.',CR,LF,0
MPNOW:  DB      'Currently selected: ',0
MPSIOM: DB      'Z80 SIO/2 channel',CR,LF
        DB      '  1  board 1 channel A - 80/81',CR,LF
        DB      '  2  board 1 channel B - 82/83  (usual AUX: port)',CR,LF
        DB      '  3  board 2 channel A - 84/85',CR,LF
        DB      '  4  board 2 channel B - 86/87',CR,LF
        DB      '  B  some other address',CR,LF
        DB      '  Q  Back  (^C also backs out)',CR,LF
        DB      CR,LF,'Choice: ',0
MPACIM: DB      '6850 ACIA port',CR,LF
        DB      '  1  88-2SIO port 1 - 10/11',CR,LF
        DB      '  2  88-2SIO port 2 - 12/13',CR,LF
        DB      '  B  some other address',CR,LF
        DB      '  Q  Back  (^C also backs out)',CR,LF
        DB      CR,LF,'Choice: ',0
MPHBM:  DB      CR,LF,'Which unit (0-3), Q to go back: ',0
MNOHB:  DB      'This machine has no RST 8 vector, so it is not',CR,LF
        DB      'running RomWBW - HBIOS is not available here.',CR,LF
        DB      'Pick the chip your board uses instead.',CR,LF,0
MNOZ180: DB     'This processor is not a Z180, so it has no ASCI',CR,LF
        DB      'ports.  Pick the serial chip your board uses.',CR,LF,0
MPASCM: DB      'Z180 ASCI channel',CR,LF
        DB      'Pick a channel: nothing is selected until you do.',CR,LF
        DB      '  0  channel 0  (ASCI0 - usually the console)',CR,LF
        DB      '  1  channel 1  (ASCI1 - the second port)',CR,LF
        DB      '  R  re-read the I/O base from RomWBW',CR,LF
        DB      '  C  I/O base C0 - RomWBW on Small Computer',CR,LF
        DB      '     boards (SC126 and friends)',CR,LF
        DB      '  B  set the internal I/O base by hand',CR,LF
        DB      '  Q  Back, leaving the port as it is',CR,LF
        DB      '     (^C also backs out)',CR,LF,0
MASCBAS: DB     'I/O base in force: ',0
MASCPR: DB      CR,LF,'Channel (0/1), or R C B Q: ',0
MASCC0: DB      'Internal I/O base set to C0.',CR,LF,0
MASCGOT: DB     'RomWBW reports the ASCI block at base ',0
MASCPIK: DB     'Now pick the channel: 0 or 1.',CR,LF,0
MASCNOR: DB     'There is no RomWBW here to ask, or it lists no ASCI',CR,LF
        DB      'port.  Press C for C0 (what RomWBW uses on Small',CR,LF
        DB      'Computer boards) or B to enter the base yourself.',CR,LF,0
MHBUNM: DB      'RomWBW HBIOS serial units',CR,LF
        DB      '-------------------------',CR,LF,0
MHBIND: DB      '  ',0
MHBBAS: DB      '   base ',0
MHBNON: DB      '(not present)',CR,LF,0
MAUXW:  DB      'CP/M AUX: selected.  Note that CP/M 2.2 has no way',CR,LF
        DB      'to ask whether a byte is waiting, so this family',CR,LF
        DB      'reads ahead and cannot receive a literal ^Z.  Use a',CR,LF
        DB      'chip family instead when one fits the machine.',CR,LF,0
MHEXA:  DB      CR,LF,'Address in hex (two digits): ',0
MPSET:  DB      'Port is now: ',0

MSETT:  DB      'Settings',CR,LF
        DB      '--------',CR,LF
        DB      'Mode:     ',0
MSPORT: DB      'Port:     ',0
MSKEY:  DB      'Menu key: ',0
MSCLS:  DB      'Clear:    ',0
MSETT2: DB      CR,LF
        DB      '  P  Serial port - which chip, which address',CR,LF
        DB      '  A  ANSI / ASCII output',CR,LF
        DB      '  E  Local echo on / off',CR,LF
        DB      '  L  Send LF after a typed CR',CR,LF
        DB      '  K  Change the menu key',CR,LF
        DB      '  B  Line settings - baud, bits, parity',CR,LF
        DB      '  C  Clear Screen',CR,LF
        DB      '  Q  Back  (^C also backs out)',CR,LF
        DB      CR,LF,'Choice: ',0
MESCAS: DB      'Press the control key you want as the menu key.',CR,LF
        DB      '(^C cancels and keeps the present one.) ',0
MESCNO: DB      'That key cannot be the menu key.  It has to be a',CR,LF
        DB      'control key, and not ^C, RETURN, LINEFEED, TAB or',CR,LF
        DB      'ESC - ^C backs out of these screens, the first',CR,LF
        DB      'three are ordinary typing, and ESC begins the',CR,LF
        DB      'arrow-key sequences, so a cursor key would open',CR,LF
        DB      'the menu.',CR,LF,0
MLINE:  DB      'Line settings',CR,LF
        DB      '-------------',CR,LF,0
MLRATE: DB      'Rate:     ',0
MLFMT:  DB      'Format:   ',0
MLINE2: DB      CR,LF
        DB      '  B  Baud rate',CR,LF
        DB      '  D  Data bits / parity / stop bits',CR,LF
        DB      '  A  Apply to the port now',CR,LF
        DB      '  R  Default - program nothing',CR,LF
        DB      '  Q  Back  (^C also backs out)',CR,LF
        DB      CR,LF,'Choice: ',0
MLRSIO: DB      'set by the board clock or CTC',0
MLRAUX: DB      'set by CP/M - the OS owns this port',0
MLRNON: DB      'no port selected',0
MLRACI: DB      'board clock ',0
MLD1:   DB      '/ 1',0
MLD16:  DB      '/ 16  (the usual setting)',0
MLD64:  DB      '/ 64',0
MLASCLK: DB     '  (at 18.432 MHz)',0
MFDATA: DB      ' data, ',0
MFPNO:  DB      'no parity, ',0
MFPEV:  DB      'even parity, ',0
MFPOD:  DB      'odd parity, ',0
MFSTOP: DB      ' stop',0
MLAPPY: DB      'EGT80 sets this on the port when it is selected.',0
MLAPPN: DB      'EGT80 programs nothing: the system setting stands.',0
MLDEF:  DB      'Back to programming nothing - 9600 8N1 shown, but',CR,LF
        DB      'not written to any port.  A chip already',CR,LF
        DB      'programmed keeps what it was given until the',CR,LF
        DB      'machine is reset.',CR,LF,0
MLOK:   DB      'Applied to the port.',CR,LF,0
MLHBNO: DB      'The firmware refused those line characteristics,',CR,LF
        DB      'or there is no RomWBW here to ask.',CR,LF,0
MLACNO: DB      'The 6850 has no such combination.  It offers 7',CR,LF
        DB      'data bits only with parity, and 8 data bits with',CR,LF
        DB      'parity only with one stop bit.',CR,LF,0
MLNOSET: DB     'This port has no line settings EGT80 can write.',CR,LF
        DB      'A Z80 SIO/2 has no baud rate generator at all -',CR,LF
        DB      'the bit rate comes from the board clock or its',CR,LF
        DB      'CTC - and its registers cannot be read back, so',CR,LF
        DB      'EGT80 will not guess at them.  CP/M AUX: belongs',CR,LF
        DB      'to the operating system.  Set the rate where the',CR,LF
        DB      'machine sets it, or use HBIOS if this is a RomWBW',CR,LF
        DB      'system.  (Against the gateway itself nothing is',CR,LF
        DB      'needed: a TCP connection has no bit rate.)',CR,LF,0
MBAUDM: DB      'Baud rate',CR,LF
        DB      '---------',CR,LF
        DB      '  A  300      F  9600',CR,LF
        DB      '  B  600      G  19200',CR,LF
        DB      '  C  1200     H  38400',CR,LF
        DB      '  D  2400     I  57600',CR,LF
        DB      '  E  4800     J  115200',CR,LF
        DB      '  Q  Back  (^C also backs out)',CR,LF
        DB      CR,LF,'Choice: ',0
MACIDM: DB      '6850 clock divider',CR,LF
        DB      '------------------',CR,LF
        DB      'A 6850 has no rate of its own: it divides the',CR,LF
        DB      'clock the board gives it.',CR,LF,CR,LF
        DB      '  1  / 1   (a synchronous clock only)',CR,LF
        DB      '  2  / 16  (the usual setting)',CR,LF
        DB      '  3  / 64  (a quarter of the / 16 rate)',CR,LF
        DB      '  Q  Back  (^C also backs out)',CR,LF
        DB      CR,LF,'Choice: ',0
MFMTM:  DB      'Data bits, parity and stop bits',CR,LF
        DB      '-------------------------------',CR,LF
        DB      'Now: ',0
MFMTM2: DB      CR,LF
        DB      '  7  7 data bits      N  No parity',CR,LF
        DB      '  8  8 data bits      E  Even parity',CR,LF
        DB      '                      O  Odd parity',CR,LF
        DB      '  1  1 stop bit',CR,LF
        DB      '  2  2 stop bits',CR,LF
        DB      '  Q  Back  (^C also backs out)',CR,LF
        DB      CR,LF,'Choice: ',0
MR300:  DB      '300',0
MR600:  DB      '600',0
MR1200: DB      '1200',0
MR2400: DB      '2400',0
MR4800: DB      '4800',0
MR9600: DB      '9600',0
MR19K:  DB      '19200',0
MR38K:  DB      '38400',0
MR57K:  DB      '57600',0
MR115K: DB      '115200',0
MCLSZ:  DB      'ADM-3A ^Z  (works through the gateway)',0
MCLSAN: DB      'ANSI ESC [ 2 J  (VT100 and modern terminals)',0
MCLSOF: DB      'off  (printing terminal - do not clear)',0
MANSCL: DB      ESC,'[2J',ESC,'[H',0

MTERM:  DB      'Terminal mode.  Press ',0
MTERM2: DB      ' for the terminal menu.',CR,LF
        DB      'To return to the main menu, press ',0
MTERM3: DB      ' then E.',CR,LF,CR,LF,0
MTEXIT: DB      'Leaving terminal mode.',CR,LF,0
MESCMN: DB      'E)xit H)elp S)et U)pload D)ownload (twice=send): ',0
MNOPRT: DB      CR,LF,'No serial port is selected yet.',CR,LF
        DB      'Press S for Settings, then P, and pick the one',CR,LF
        DB      'your machine uses.',CR,LF,0

MDEAD:  DB      'Nothing was sent: the port did not accept the',CR,LF
        DB      'character.  Selected port is ',0
MDEAD2: DB      'Either no serial chip answers at that address,',CR,LF
        DB      'or it is held off by flow control.  Press S for',CR,LF
        DB      'Settings, then P, to pick a different port.',CR,LF,0

MXSFIL: DB      CR,LF,'Send which file? ',0
MXRFIL: DB      CR,LF,'Receive as which file? ',0
MXNONAM: DB     'No filename given - nothing done.',CR,LF,0
MXNOFIL: DB     CR,LF,'No such file on that drive.',CR,LF,0
MXEXIST: DB     CR,LF,'That file exists.  Replace it (Y/N)?  The old',CR,LF
        DB      'one is deleted before the transfer starts. ',0
MXKEEP: DB      'Kept the existing file - nothing done.',CR,LF,0
MXNOMK: DB      CR,LF,'Could not create the file (directory full?).',CR,LF,0
MXWAIT: DB      CR,LF,'Waiting for the other end to start receiving...',CR,LF,0
MXGO:   DB      CR,LF,'Transferring ( . = block, ? = retry, ^C aborts )',CR,LF,0
MXSENT: DB      'Sent.',CR,LF,0
MXRECVD: DB     'Received.',CR,LF,0
MXFAIL: DB      'Transfer failed - the other end stopped answering.',CR,LF,0
MXCAN:  DB      'The other end cancelled the transfer.',CR,LF,0
MXSCRAP: DB     'The incomplete file has been deleted.',CR,LF,0
MXUSER: DB      'Stopped at this end.',CR,LF,0
MXDISK: DB      'Disk full or write failed - transfer stopped.',CR,LF,0

MUNSAVE: DB    CR,LF,'  (changed - press V to keep it for next time)',CR,LF
        DB      CR,LF,'Choice: ',0
MSAVED: DB      'Settings saved into EGT80.COM.',CR,LF,0
MSAVERR: DB     'Could not save: EGT80.COM must be on the current',CR,LF
        DB      'drive under its own name.  Settings still apply',CR,LF
        DB      'for this run.',CR,LF,0
MCFGBAD: DB     'Saved settings were not valid - defaults restored.',CR,LF,0
CFGREF: DB      'EGT80CFG'      ; what CFGSIG must contain

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; CFGFCB - a fixed FCB for our own file.  CP/M never tells a program its
; own name, so this is the one place EGT80.COM is hard-coded; a renamed or
; off-drive copy makes the save fail with a message instead of writing to
; the wrong file.  Drive byte 0 = whichever drive is current.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
CFGFCB: DB      0
        DB      'EGT80   '
        DB      'COM'
        DB      0,0,0,0
        DB      0,0,0,0,0,0,0,0
        DB      0,0,0,0,0,0,0,0
        DB      0               ; cr
        DB      0,0,0           ; r0/r1/r2 - the random record number

MKEY:   DB      CR,LF,'Press any key. ',0
MCTRLC: DB      CR,LF,'Press Ctrl-C for the menu.  Anything else you',CR,LF
        DB      'type here is ignored, so a half-typed line cannot',CR,LF
        DB      'run a menu command by accident. ',0
MBYE:   DB      'EGT80 done.',CR,LF,0

MHELP:  DB      'Help - the serial port',CR,LF
        DB      '----------------------',CR,LF,CR,LF
        DB      'Choosing the port is the one setting that decides',CR,LF
        DB      'whether EGT80 works.  Get it wrong and nothing is',CR,LF
        DB      'sent and nothing arrives - no error, just silence.',CR,LF,CR,LF
        DB      'Z80 SIO/2   RC2014 and most Z80 boards.  On RC2014',CR,LF
        DB      '            or RomWBW, 82/83 is the usual channel.',CR,LF
        DB      '6850 ACIA   Altair 88-2SIO (10/11, 12/13) and the',CR,LF
        DB      '            RC2014 ACIA boards.',CR,LF
        DB      'RomWBW      Asks the firmware to move the byte, so',CR,LF
        DB      'HBIOS       it works on any RomWBW machine whatever',CR,LF
        DB      '            the hardware.  Needs RomWBW.',CR,LF
        DB      'Z180 ASCI   The serial ports inside a Z180, as on',CR,LF
        DB      '            an SC126.',CR,LF
        DB      'CP/M AUX:   Portable fallback - read help page 3',CR,LF
        DB      '            before choosing it.',CR,LF,0
MHELP2: DB      'Help - using the terminal',CR,LF
        DB      '-------------------------',CR,LF,CR,LF
        DB      'Terminal mode passes what you type to the port and',CR,LF
        DB      'what arrives to your screen.  The menu key',CR,LF
        DB      'interrupts it; pressing it twice sends it to the',CR,LF
        DB      'remote end instead.',CR,LF
        DB      'Settings holds the rest: the serial port, the line',CR,LF
        DB      'settings (speed and framing, where the hardware has',CR,LF
        DB      'any to set - a Z80 SIO/2 has none), the menu key',CR,LF
        DB      '(any control key you press), and how to clear the',CR,LF
        DB      'screen, which CP/M has no standard way to do.',CR,LF,CR,LF
        DB      'ANSI mode passes escape sequences through to your',CR,LF
        DB      'terminal.  ASCII mode strips them, drops the high',CR,LF
        DB      'bit and discards control codes that are not CR, LF,',CR,LF
        DB      'TAB, BS or BEL - which suits a printing terminal or',CR,LF
        DB      'a console with no cursor addressing: a remote menu',CR,LF
        DB      'drawn with graphics still reads as text.',CR,LF,CR,LF
        DB      'Settings are saved into EGT80.COM itself, so a copy',CR,LF
        DB      'carries them to another disk - the file must be on',CR,LF
        DB      'the current drive under its own name.',CR,LF,0
MHELP3: DB      'Help - two things to know',CR,LF
        DB      '-------------------------',CR,LF,CR,LF
        DB      'CP/M AUX: has no way to ask whether a byte is',CR,LF
        DB      'waiting - the OS offers no such call - so EGT80',CR,LF
        DB      'reads one byte ahead.  This family therefore cannot',CR,LF
        DB      'receive a literal ^Z, and on real CP/M 2.2 the read',CR,LF
        DB      'waits instead of returning.  Against the gateway it',CR,LF
        DB      'works well; on real hardware prefer the family that',CR,LF
        DB      'matches your serial chip.',CR,LF,CR,LF
        DB      'The Z180 ASCI registers live inside the CPU, and',CR,LF
        DB      'the block moves: RomWBW puts it at C0 on Small',CR,LF
        DB      'Computer boards.  Press C for C0, or B to type',CR,LF
        DB      'another, then pick the channel.',CR,LF,CR,LF
        DB      'Transfers are plain XMODEM, 128-byte blocks, CRC',CR,LF
        DB      'with the checksum fallback an older peer may want.',CR,LF
        DB      'Start the far end first, then press U or D - from',CR,LF
        DB      'the menu or the terminal-mode menu key, which does',CR,LF
        DB      'not hang up.  A dot is a block, a ? a retry.  ^C',CR,LF
        DB      'stops a transfer at any point, and at the filename',CR,LF
        DB      'prompt too; an incomplete download is deleted',CR,LF
        DB      'rather than left looking like a file.',CR,LF,0

;=======================================================================
; VECTORS AND VARIABLES
;
; The vectors are JP instructions rewritten at run time; the 0 operands
; below are placeholders that CONSET and PORTST fill in before anything
; can call them.
;=======================================================================
VCST:   JP      0               ; console status
VCIN:   JP      0               ; console input
VCOUT:  JP      0               ; console output
VPST:   JP      0               ; port receive status
VPIN:   JP      0               ; port input
VPOST:  JP      0               ; port transmit status
VPOUT:  JP      0               ; port output

CPMVER: DB      0               ; BDOS version, from CONSET
FSTATE: DB      0               ; inbound escape-sequence state
ESTATE: DB      0               ; local-echo escape-sequence state
SENDCH: DB      0               ; byte in flight in PSEND
PTRIES: DB      0               ; PSEND give-up counter
PDEAD:  DB      0               ; set when the transmitter never freed
TYPECH: DB      0               ; byte in flight in TSEND
HEXACC: DB      0               ; high nibble held while HEXASK reads the low
XBLK:   DB      0               ; block number being sent / expected
XGOTBL: DB      0               ; block number as it arrived
XCRCM:  DB      0               ; non-zero = CRC mode, zero = checksum
XCRC:   DW      0               ; CRC-16 accumulator
XSUM:   DB      0               ; checksum accumulator
XCNT:   DB      0               ; retries left
XNCNT:  DB      0               ; junk bytes still tolerated
XRXACT: DB      0               ; non-zero while a download's file exists
XDIRTY: DB      0               ; non-zero when settings differ from the file
XTMO:   DB      0               ; XGETB timeout, in 65536-poll passes
XTMOC:  DB      0               ; ...and its working copy
XLINE:  DS      24              ; console line buffer for a filename
XFCB:   DS      36              ; FCB for the file being transferred
XBUF:   DS      128             ; the block, which is also the CP/M record
LSQUIT: DB      0               ; non-zero while an apply must not print
HBUNIT: DB      0               ; unit number handed to HBDEV
HBTYPE: DB      0               ; device type it reported
HBPHYS: DB      0               ; physical channel it reported
HBBASE: DB      0               ; base I/O address it reported
HBSCAN: DB      0               ; unit being listed / scanned
AUXHLD: DB      0               ; byte read ahead by the AUX status call
AUXFLG: DB      0               ; non-zero while AUXHLD holds a byte

; Stack.  128 bytes = 64 levels, which is more than this program's own
; call depth needs (about a dozen levels at the deepest, inside a
; transfer) - the margin is for the system underneath: a real CP/M BIOS
; may use the CALLER's stack, and the BDOS file calls a transfer makes
; are exactly where that would bite.  It matters here because the stack
; grows down towards XBUF, so an overflow would quietly corrupt the block
; being transferred rather than crashing where it happened.
        DS      128
STKTOP  EQU     $

        END     START
